window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-phy-units","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Units and Measurements · Practice","chapter":"Units and Measurements","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A distance is measured as $7.2 \\times 10^{5}$ m. Using the NCERT convention for estimating order of magnitude, the order of magnitude of this distance is:","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: the number is $a \\times 10^b$ with $a = 7.2$ and $b = 5$.\nRule: round $a$ to 1 if $a \\le 5$, and to 10 if $5 < a \\le 10$.\nSince $7.2 > 5$, $a$ becomes 10. The value is then about $10 \\times 10^5 = 10^6$ m.\nSo the order of magnitude is 6, not 5. Choosing 5 is the common trap of ignoring the rounding of $a$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The length and breadth of a rectangular sheet are measured as $l = 20.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm and $b = 12.5 \\pm 0.1$ cm. Using the rule for combining errors in a product, what is the maximum absolute error in the area $lb$?","image":"","options":["0.2 cm$^2$","3.25 cm$^2$","1.3 cm$^2$","2.5 cm$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $l = 20.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm and $b = 12.5 \\pm 0.1$ cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{\\Delta A}{A} = \\frac{\\Delta l}{l} + \\frac{\\Delta b}{b}$.\nSubstituting: 0.5 % + 0.8 % = 1.3 %. The area is $A = 250$ cm$^2$.\n$\\Delta A = 0.013 \\times 250 = 3.25$ cm$^2$, so the result is quoted as $250 \\pm 3$ cm$^2$.\nOption 1.3 is the percentage error, not the absolute error.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The velocity $v$ of a particle varies with time $t$ as $v = at + \\frac{b}{t + c}$, where $a$, $b$ and $c$ are constants. The dimensional formula of $\\frac{ab}{c}$ is:","image":"","options":["$[L T^{-3}]$","$[L^2 T^{-2}]$","$[L^2 T^{-3}]$","$[L T^{-1}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Only like quantities can be added, so $c$ must have the dimensions of time: $[c] = [T]$.\nFrom the first term, $[a][T] = [L T^{-1}]$, so $[a] = [L T^{-2}]$.\nFrom the second term, $[b]/[T] = [L T^{-1}]$, so $[b] = [L]$.\nThen $[ab/c] = [L T^{-2}][L]/[T] = [L^2 T^{-3}]$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A cone has its apex at the centre O of a sphere of radius 2 m. The cone cuts out an area of 0.5 m$^2$ on the surface of the sphere. What is the solid angle the cone subtends at O?","image":"","options":["0.125 sr","0.25 sr","0.0625 sr","1.0 sr"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: intercepted area $dA = 0.5$ m$^2$, radius $r = 2$ m.\nFormula: $d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$.\nSubstituting: $d\\Omega = \\frac{0.5}{(2)^2} = \\frac{0.5}{4}$.\nSo $d\\Omega = 0.125$ sr.\nOption (2), 0.25 sr, comes from dividing by $r$ instead of $r^2$. That is the rule for a plane angle, not a solid angle.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about significant figures is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The number of significant figures in a measurement depends on the least count of the measuring instrument","The value 0.004700 km has only two significant figures, because the trailing zeros in a number less than 1 are not significant","$6.0 \\times 10^{3}$ m and 6000 m (written without a decimal point) do not have the same number of significant figures","In $T = \\frac{t}{n}$, where n is the counted number of oscillations, n is an exact number"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: significant figures indicate the precision, and the precision depends on the least count.\n(2) Incorrect: in 0.004700, only the leading zeros are insignificant. The trailing zeros in a number with a decimal point are significant, so it has 4 significant figures, just like 4.700 m.\n(3) Correct: $6.0 \\times 10^{3}$ has 2, while 6000 without a decimal has 1.\n(4) Correct: a counted number n is exact and has infinite significant figures.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The method of dimensions gives $T = k\\sqrt{\\frac{l}{g}}$ for a simple pendulum but cannot give the value $k = 2\\pi$.\nReason (R): Dimensionless constants do not affect the dimensions of an expression.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Dimensional analysis fixes only the powers of l and g. The constant has to come from experiment or from a full theory.\nR is true. A pure number such as $2\\pi$ has no dimensions, so it leaves both sides of the dimensional equation unchanged.\nBecause k is invisible in the dimensional equation, the method cannot determine it. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In the revised SI, each base unit is defined by fixing the numerical value of a constant. Match the constant in List-I with the base unit it defines in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Speed of light in vacuum, $c$\n(b) Elementary charge, $e$\n(c) Boltzmann constant, $k$\n(d) Luminous efficacy of 540 $\\times 10^{12}$ Hz radiation, $K_{cd}$\nList-II\n(i) kelvin\n(ii) candela\n(iii) metre\n(iv) ampere","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) $c$ is fixed in m s$^{-1}$, so it defines the metre: (iii).\n(b) $e$ is fixed in C = A s, so it defines the ampere: (iv).\n(c) The Boltzmann constant $k$ is fixed in J K$^{-1}$, so it defines the kelvin: (i).\n(d) $K_{cd}$ is fixed as 683 lm W$^{-1}$ = cd sr W$^{-1}$, so it defines the candela: (ii).\nOption (1) swaps $e$ and $k$, which is the most tempting slip.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Both 2.745 and 2.735, when rounded off to three significant figures, give 2.74.\nReason (R): When the insignificant digit to be dropped is 5, the preceding digit is left unchanged if it is even and raised by 1 if it is odd.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: it states NCERT's convention for a dropped digit of 5.\nIn 2.745, the preceding digit 4 is even, so the 5 is dropped → 2.74.\nIn 2.735, the preceding digit 3 is odd, so it is raised to 4 → 2.74.\nSo A is true, and it follows directly from R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"If momentum (P), area (A) and time (T) are taken to be fundamental quantities, then energy has the dimensional formula","image":"","options":["$(P^{1}A^{-1}T^{1})$","$(P^{2}A^{1}T^{1})$","$(P^{1}A^{-1/2}T^{1})$","$(P^{1}A^{1/2}T^{-1})$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: energy $[ML^2T^{-2}]$, momentum $P = [MLT^{-1}]$, area $A = [L^2]$, time $[T]$.\nAssume: $[ML^2T^{-2}] = [MLT^{-1}]^a [L^2]^b [T]^c$.\nCompare powers. M: $a = 1$. L: $a + 2b = 2$, so $b = \\tfrac{1}{2}$. T: $-a + c = -2$, so $c = -1$.\nSo energy $= P^1 A^{1/2} T^{-1}$.\nCheck: $P \\sqrt{A} / T = (\\text{momentum} \\times \\text{length}) / \\text{time} = \\text{momentum} \\times \\text{velocity}$, which has the dimensions of energy.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2.12)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the expression $y = a\\sin(kx)$, where $x$ is a distance, the constant $k$ must have dimensions $[L^{-1}]$.\nReason (R): The argument of a trigonometric function must be dimensionless.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. NCERT states that the arguments of trigonometric, logarithmic and exponential functions must be dimensionless.\nA is true. For $kx$ to be dimensionless, we need $[k][L] = [M^0L^0T^0]$, which gives $[k] = [L^{-1}]$.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about scientific notation $a \\times 10^b$ is correct?","image":"","options":["$a$ may be any positive number, but $b$ must be a positive integer","Rewriting 4.700 m as $4.700 \\times 10^{3}$ mm increases the number of significant figures from 4 to 7","All zeros in the base number $a$ are significant, and the power of 10 has no bearing on the number of significant figures","The order of magnitude of $1.28 \\times 10^{7}$ m is 1.28"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: $a$ lies between 1 and 10, and $b$ can be positive or negative.\n(2) Incorrect: changing units never changes the number of significant figures. Both forms have 4.\n(3) Correct: in scientific notation, the trailing zeros in $a$ are always significant, and the power of 10 is irrelevant to the count.\n(4) Incorrect: the order of magnitude is the exponent after rounding $a$, which here is 7.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (measured value) with List-II (number of significant figures).\nList-I:\n(A) 0.007 m\n(B) $2.640 \\times 10^{24}$ kg\n(C) 30500 m (no decimal point)\n(D) 30.500 s\nList-II:\n(i) 1\n(ii) 3\n(iii) 4\n(iv) 5","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) In 0.007, only the digit 7 is significant, so it has 1. This matches (i).\n(B) In scientific notation, all digits in the base number count, giving 2, 6, 4, 0. This is 4, matching (iii).\n(C) 30500 has no decimal point, so its trailing zeros are not significant. The digits 3, 0, 5 count, giving 3 and matching (ii).\n(D) 30.500 has a decimal point, so its trailing zeros count. That gives 5, matching (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about significant figures is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The zero written to the left of the decimal point in 0.1250 is not significant, but the zero at its end is significant","In 30.05 g the two zeros are significant, but in 3005 g they are not, because 3005 has no decimal point","In the relation $T = \\frac{t}{n}$ for the time period obtained from n counted oscillations, n is an exact number with infinitely many significant figures","Reporting a measured value with more digits than its significant figures is misleading, as it suggests a precision that the instrument does not have"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. The conventional 0 placed before the decimal in a number less than 1 is never significant, but trailing zeros in such a number are significant.\n(2) Incorrect. Zeros between two non-zero digits are always significant, wherever the decimal point is or even if there is none. So 3005 g has 4 significant figures, just like 30.05 g. The 'no decimal point' rule applies only to trailing zeros.\n(3) Correct. n is counted, not measured, so it is exact.\n(4) Correct. Extra digits are superfluous and give a wrong idea of precision.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A student reports the time period of a simple pendulum as 3.47 s using a stopwatch. Which of the following correctly describes this reported value?","image":"","options":["Two significant figures; the digits 3 and 4 are both uncertain","Three significant figures; the digits 3 and 4 are reliable and the digit 7 is uncertain","Three significant figures; all three digits are known with certainty","Two significant figures; the digit 7 is superfluous and should be dropped"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A reported measurement contains all the reliably known digits plus the first uncertain digit.\nAll of these together are the significant figures.\nIn 3.47 s, the digits 3 and 4 are reliable and the last digit, 7, is the first uncertain one.\nSo the value has three significant figures. Option (3) fails because the last digit is never certain.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A student proposes the following expressions for the kinetic energy $K$ of a body of mass $m$. Here $v$ is speed, $a$ is acceleration, $t$ is time, $s$ is displacement and $p$ is momentum.\n(i) $K = \\frac{3}{8}mv^2$\n(ii) $K = mat$\n(iii) $K = \\frac{p^2}{2m}$\n(iv) $K = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + mv$\n(v) $K = mas$\n(vi) $K = m^2v^2$\nHow many of these can be ruled out on dimensional grounds alone?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Kinetic energy has dimensions $[ML^2T^{-2}]$.\n(i) $[ML^2T^{-2}]$: not ruled out. The constant may be wrong, but dimensions cannot show it.\n(ii) $[MLT^{-1}]$: ruled out.\n(iii) $[MLT^{-1}]^2/[M] = [ML^2T^{-2}]$: not ruled out.\n(iv) Adds terms of unlike dimensions: ruled out.\n(v) $[M][LT^{-2}][L] = [ML^2T^{-2}]$: not ruled out.\n(vi) $[M^2L^2T^{-2}]$: ruled out.\nThree are ruled out.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The solid angle $d\\Omega$ subtended at the apex O is defined as the ratio of the intercepted area $dA$ of a spherical surface, centred at O, to the square of its radius $r$.\nStatement II: Both the radian and the steradian are dimensionless.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT defines $d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$, with unit steradian (sr).\nStatement II: Correct. The plane angle $d\\theta = \\frac{ds}{r}$ is length/length and the solid angle is area/area. Both are ratios of similar quantities, so both are dimensionless.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following physical quantities is dimensionless?","image":"","options":["Speed of light in vacuum","Refractive index","Acceleration due to gravity","Mass density"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Refractive index = (speed of light in vacuum)/(speed of light in medium).\nIt is a ratio of two similar quantities, $[LT^{-1}]/[LT^{-1}]$, so it has no dimensions.\nThe speed of light itself has dimensions $[LT^{-1}]$, so option (1) is wrong.\n$g$ has dimensions $[LT^{-2}]$ and density has $[ML^{-3}]$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The speed v of a transverse wave on a stretched string is assumed to depend on the tension F and the mass per unit length $\\mu$ as $v = k F^a \\mu^b$. Using dimensions, the values of a and b are:","image":"","options":["$a = \\frac{1}{2},\\ b = \\frac{1}{2}$","$a = \\frac{1}{2},\\ b = -\\frac{1}{2}$","$a = 1,\\ b = -1$","$a = -\\frac{1}{2},\\ b = \\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Dimensions: $[v] = [L T^{-1}]$, $[F] = [M L T^{-2}]$, $[\\mu] = [M L^{-1}]$.\nThe right side becomes $[M^{a+b} L^{a-b} T^{-2a}]$.\nPowers of T: $-2a = -1$, so $a = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nPowers of M: $a + b = 0$, so $b = -\\frac{1}{2}$.\nCheck L: $a - b = 1$, which matches.\nSo $v = k\\sqrt{\\frac{F}{\\mu}}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (physical quantity) with List-II (dimensional formula).\nList-I:\n(a) Volume\n(b) Speed\n(c) Force\n(d) Acceleration\nList-II:\n(i) $[M L T^{-2}]$\n(ii) $[M^0 L T^{-2}]$\n(iii) $[M^0 L^3 T^0]$\n(iv) $[M^0 L T^{-1}]$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Volume = length × breadth × height, so it is $[M^0L^3T^0]$, which is (iii).\n(b) Speed = length/time, so it is $[M^0LT^{-1}]$, which is (iv).\n(c) Force = mass × acceleration, so it is $[MLT^{-2}]$, which is (i).\n(d) Acceleration = length/time$^2$, so it is $[M^0LT^{-2}]$, which is (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The length and breadth of a rectangular plate are measured as $l = 20.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm and $b = 5.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm. The percentage error in the calculated area $lb$ is:","image":"","options":["0.5 %","2.0 %","2.5 %","1.5 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta l = 0.1$ cm on $l = 20.0$ cm, and $\\Delta b = 0.1$ cm on $b = 5.0$ cm.\nFormula: for a product, the percentage errors add: $\\frac{\\Delta A}{A} \\times 100 = \\left(\\frac{\\Delta l}{l} + \\frac{\\Delta b}{b}\\right) \\times 100$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{\\Delta l}{l} \\times 100 = 0.5\\%$ and $\\frac{\\Delta b}{b} \\times 100 = 2.0\\%$.\nSo the percentage error in the area = 0.5% + 2.0% = 2.5%.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Quantity):\n(A) Force\n(B) Pressure\n(C) Work\n(D) Momentum\nList-II (Dimensional formula):\n(I) $[M L T^{-1}]$\n(II) $[M L T^{-2}]$\n(III) $[M L^{-1} T^{-2}]$\n(IV) $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Force = mass × acceleration, so $[M L T^{-2}]$. This matches II.\n(B) Pressure = force/area, so $[M L T^{-2}]/[L^2] = [M L^{-1} T^{-2}]$. This matches III.\n(C) Work = force × distance, so $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$. This matches IV.\n(D) Momentum = mass × velocity, so $[M L T^{-1}]$. This matches I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Initial velocity, average velocity, change in velocity and speed all have the same dimensional formula.\nReason (R): A dimensional formula shows only the type of physical quantity and does not take its magnitude into account.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: each of these quantities is length/time, so each has dimensions $[LT^{-1}]$.\nR is true: NCERT states that magnitudes are not considered in a dimensional formula; only the type (quality) of the quantity matters.\nR explains A. Quantities that differ only in value or context therefore share one dimensional formula.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Following the NCERT rounding convention, the numbers 6.385 and 6.375 are each rounded off to three significant figures. The results are, respectively:","image":"","options":["6.39 and 6.38","6.38 and 6.38","6.38 and 6.37","6.39 and 6.37"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When the digit to be dropped is exactly 5, look at the digit before it.\n6.385: the preceding digit 8 is even, so the 5 is simply dropped, giving 6.38.\n6.375: the preceding digit 7 is odd, so it is raised by 1, giving 6.38.\nBoth numbers therefore round to 6.38. Always rounding a 5 upward would wrongly give 6.39 for the first number.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the SI base unit of luminous intensity, with its correct symbol?","image":"","options":["lumen (lm)","candela (cd)","lux (lx)","watt (W)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Luminous intensity in a given direction is one of the seven SI base quantities.\nIts base unit is the candela, symbol cd.\nThe lumen appears in the definition of the candela, through the luminous efficacy in lm W$^{-1}$, but it is a derived unit and not a base unit.\nThe lux and the watt are also derived units.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Of the seven dimensions of the physical world, which base quantity is represented by the symbol [K]?","image":"","options":["Luminous intensity","Amount of substance","Thermodynamic temperature","Electric current"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT gives the seven dimensions as: [L] length, [M] mass, [T] time, [A] electric current, [K] thermodynamic temperature, [cd] luminous intensity and [mol] amount of substance.\nSo [K] stands for thermodynamic temperature.\nElectric current is [A], not [K], so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In the revised SI definitions, which pair of base units is defined without requiring any other base unit to be defined first?","image":"","options":["metre and second","kilogram and mole","second and mole","ampere and kelvin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Second: fixed only by $\\Delta\\nu_{Cs}$ in Hz = s$^{-1}$, so it needs no other base unit.\nMole: fixed only by the exact count $6.02214076\\times10^{23}$ entities, so it is also independent.\nMetre needs the second, and the kilogram needs the metre and second.\nAmpere needs the second, and the kelvin needs kg, m and s.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are SI base units?\nnewton, kelvin, candela, radian, mole, joule, ampere, litre","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Base units in the list: kelvin, candela, mole and ampere, which makes 4.\nNot base units:\n- Newton and joule are derived units with special names.\n- Radian is the dimensionless unit of plane angle.\n- Litre is a unit retained outside SI.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following results are correctly reported according to the rules for significant figures? (All numbers are measured values.)\n(a) 2.5 × 1.25 = 3.1\n(b) 12.6 + 0.375 = 12.98\n(c) 9.80 − 9.7 = 0.1\n(d) $(3.00 \\times 10^{8}) \\times (2.000 \\times 10^{2}) = 6.00 \\times 10^{10}$\n(e) 7.4 ÷ 2.000 = 3.70","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: 3.125 is limited to 2 significant figures by 2.5, giving 3.1.\n(b) Incorrect: 12.975 must have 1 decimal place, giving 13.0, not 12.98.\n(c) Correct: 0.10 is limited to 1 decimal place, giving 0.1.\n(d) Correct: the result is limited to 3 significant figures, giving $6.00 \\times 10^{10}$.\n(e) Incorrect: 7.4 has only 2 significant figures, so the result is 3.7, not 3.70.\nThree results, (a), (c) and (d), are correctly reported.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"You measure two quantities as A = 1.0 m $\\pm$ 0.2 m, B = 2.0 m $\\pm$ 0.2 m. We should report correct value for $\\sqrt{AB}$ as:","image":"","options":["1.4 m $\\pm$ 0.4 m","1.41 m $\\pm$ 0.15 m","1.4 m $\\pm$ 0.3 m","1.4 m $\\pm$ 0.2 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 1.0 \\pm 0.2$ m and $B = 2.0 \\pm 0.2$ m.\nFormula: for $\\sqrt{AB}$, the relative error is $\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\Delta A}{A} + \\frac{\\Delta B}{B}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $\\sqrt{2.0} \\approx 1.414$ m. The relative error is $\\frac{1}{2}(0.2 + 0.1) = 0.15$, so the absolute error is $0.15 \\times 1.414 \\approx 0.21$ m.\nThe error has one significant figure, so the result is rounded to the first decimal place: $1.4 \\pm 0.2$ m.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2.8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is a derived unit, and not one of the SI base units?","image":"","options":["kelvin","mole","newton","candela"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Kelvin (temperature), mole (amount of substance) and candela (luminous intensity) are among the seven SI base units.\nThe newton is the unit of force.\nIt is built from base units as kg m s$^{-2}$, so it is a derived unit with a special name.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In the dimensional derivation of the period of a simple pendulum, $T = k\\,l^x g^y m^z$. What does equating the powers of [M] on both sides show?","image":"","options":["$z = 1$, so the period is directly proportional to the mass of the bob","$z = 0$, so the period does not depend on the mass of the bob","$z = -\\frac{1}{2}$, so a heavier bob oscillates faster","$z$ cannot be found because mass appears in no other variable"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The left side, [T], has zero power of [M]. On the right side, only $m^z$ carries mass, giving $[M^z]$.\nEquating powers of M gives $z = 0$, so $m^0 = 1$ and mass drops out of the formula.\nOption (4) fails because z is found easily: mass appears only through m, and [T] contains no mass.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following measured values have exactly three significant figures?\n0.00230 kg, $2.30 \\times 10^{4}$ m, 4500 m, 0.0405 s, 6.020 J, 300.0 K, 1.07 A, 5.0 cm","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Three significant figures:\n- 0.00230: the digits 2, 3 and the trailing 0 count.\n- $2.30\\times10^4$: in scientific notation, the zero in the base number is significant.\n- 0.0405: the zero lies between non-zero digits.\n- 1.07: the zero lies between non-zero digits.\nOther counts:\n- 4500 has 2, since trailing zeros with no decimal point are not significant.\n- 6.020 has 4.\n- 300.0 has 4.\n- 5.0 has 2.\nTotal with exactly three = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The frequency f of small oscillations of a liquid drop is assumed to depend on the surface tension S (dimensions $[M T^{-2}]$), the density $\\rho$ and the radius r of the drop. By dimensional analysis, f is proportional to:","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{\\frac{S}{\\rho r^3}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{S r^3}{\\rho}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{\\rho r^3}{S}}$","$\\frac{S}{\\rho r^2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $f = k S^a \\rho^b r^c$, so $[T^{-1}] = [M T^{-2}]^a [M L^{-3}]^b [L]^c$.\nPowers of T: $-2a = -1$, so $a = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nPowers of M: $a + b = 0$, so $b = -\\frac{1}{2}$.\nPowers of L: $-3b + c = 0$, so $c = -\\frac{3}{2}$.\nTherefore $f \\propto S^{1/2}\\rho^{-1/2}r^{-3/2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{S}{\\rho r^3}}$.\nOption (3) is the time period, not the frequency.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A student recalls the time dilation relation of special relativity as $t = \\frac{t_0}{\\sqrt{1 - v^2}}$. Here $t_0$ and $t$ are time intervals, $v$ is the speed of a particle, and he has forgotten where the speed of light $c$ appears. Using dimensional reasoning, the correct form is:","image":"","options":["$t = \\frac{t_0}{\\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}}$","$t = \\frac{t_0}{\\sqrt{c^2 - v^2}}$","$t = \\frac{t_0\\,c}{\\sqrt{1 - v^2}}$","$t = \\frac{t_0}{\\sqrt{1 - v/c^2}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The number 1 can only be subtracted from a dimensionless quantity, so $v^2$ must be divided by $c^2$.\nThe ratio $v^2/c^2$ is dimensionless, and $t$ and $t_0$ are both times. So option (1) is consistent.\n(2) gives $[T]/[LT^{-1}] = [L^{-1}T^2]$, which is not a time.\n(3) still subtracts $[L^2T^{-2}]$ from 1.\n(4) has $v/c^2$ with dimensions $[L^{-1}T]$, which is not dimensionless.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following measurements is most precise?","image":"","options":["5.00 mm","5.00 cm","5.00 m","5.00 km."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Precision depends on the smallest absolute uncertainty, which is the least count of the measurement.\nEach reading has three significant figures, so the uncertainty is in the second decimal place of its unit:\n5.00 mm gives ±0.01 mm. 5.00 cm gives ±0.01 cm. 5.00 m gives ±0.01 m. 5.00 km gives ±0.01 km.\nThe uncertainty of 0.01 mm is the smallest, so 5.00 mm is the most precise.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2.9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The diameter of the earth ($1.28 \\times 10^{7}$ m) is 17 orders of magnitude larger than the diameter of a hydrogen atom ($1.06 \\times 10^{-10}$ m).\nReason (R): The order of magnitude of the earth's diameter is 7 and that of the hydrogen atom's diameter is $-10$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. For the earth, $a = 1.28 \\le 5$ rounds to 1, so the diameter is about $10^7$ m (order 7). For the atom, $a = 1.06$ rounds to 1, so the diameter is about $10^{-10}$ m (order $-10$).\nA is true. The difference in orders is $7 - (-10) = 17$.\nA follows directly from the orders given in R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the SI base units is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["When the mole is used, the elementary entities must be specified, and such an entity may even be a specified group of particles.","The candela is defined using the luminous efficacy of monochromatic radiation of frequency $540\\times10^{12}$ Hz, and its definition needs the kilogram, metre and second.","The kelvin is defined by fixing the Boltzmann constant in J K$^{-1}$, and its definition requires only the second to be defined beforehand.","The amount of substance $n$ of a system is a measure of the number of specified elementary entities in it."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: entities must be specified, and they may be atoms, molecules, ions, electrons or specified groups of particles.\n(2) Correct: $K_{cd}$ is expressed in cd sr kg$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$ s$^3$, so it needs kg, m and s.\n(3) Incorrect: J K$^{-1}$ = kg m$^2$ s$^{-2}$ K$^{-1}$, so the kelvin needs the kilogram, metre and second, not just the second.\n(4) Correct: this is how amount of substance is defined.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A measured length is $4.9 \\times 10^{-3}$ m. Using the NCERT convention for rounding the base number, what is its order of magnitude?","image":"","options":["$-3$","$-2$","$-4$","$3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write the number as $a \\times 10^b$ with $a = 4.9$ and $b = -3$.\nNCERT rule: round $a$ to 1 if $a \\le 5$, and to 10 if $5 < a \\le 10$.\nHere $4.9 \\le 5$, so $a$ rounds to 1 and the length is about $10^{-3}$ m.\nThe order of magnitude is therefore $-3$. Option (2) would apply only if $a$ were greater than 5.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The result of 24.6 cm − 18.32 cm should be reported as 6.3 cm.\nReason (R): In subtraction, the result must retain as many significant figures as the number having the least significant figures.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The difference is 6.28 cm. Since 24.6 cm has only one decimal place, the result is rounded to one decimal place, giving 6.3 cm.\nR is false. For addition and subtraction, the rule is based on decimal places, not on significant figures.\nApplying R would keep 3 significant figures (6.28 cm), which contradicts A.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Young’s modulus of steel is 1.9 $\\times$ 10$^{11}$ N/m$^{2}$. When expressed in CGS units of dynes/cm$^{2}$, it will be equal to (1 N = 10$^{5}$ dyne, 1 m$^{2}$ = 10$^{4}$ cm$^{2}$)","image":"","options":["1.9 $\\times$ 10$^{10}$","1.9 $\\times$ 10$^{11}$","1.9 $\\times$ 10$^{12}$","1.9 $\\times$ 10$^{13}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $Y = 1.9 \\times 10^{11}$ N/m$^2$, with $1$ N $= 10^5$ dyne and $1$ m$^2 = 10^4$ cm$^2$.\nConversion: $1\\ \\text{N/m}^2 = \\dfrac{10^5\\ \\text{dyne}}{10^4\\ \\text{cm}^2} = 10\\ \\text{dyne/cm}^2$.\nSubstituting: $Y = 1.9 \\times 10^{11} \\times 10\\ \\text{dyne/cm}^2$.\nSo $Y = 1.9 \\times 10^{12}$ dyne/cm$^2$.\nCommon slip: multiplying the force factor by the area factor instead of dividing gives $10^{20}$, which is not offered. Using the force factor alone gives $1.9 \\times 10^{16}$, which is also absent.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2.11)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Each edge of a cubical tank is measured as 4.12 m. Which option gives its total surface area and its volume to the appropriate significant figures?","image":"","options":["101.8 $m^2$ and 69.93 $m^3$","$1.0 \\times 10^{2}$ $m^2$ and 70 $m^3$","102 $m^2$ and 69.9 $m^3$","101.85 $m^2$ and 69.935 $m^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: edge a = 4.12 m, which has 3 significant figures.\nThe factor 6 in the surface area $6a^2$ is exact, so it does not limit precision.\nSurface area = $6(4.12)^2$ = 101.8464 $m^2$, which rounds to 102 $m^2$.\nVolume = $(4.12)^3$ = 69.934528 $m^3$, which rounds to 69.9 $m^3$.\nOption 2 wrongly treats 6 as having one significant figure.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A small patch of area 0.030 m$^2$ lies on the surface of a sphere of radius 0.50 m. What solid angle does the patch subtend at the centre of the sphere?","image":"","options":["0.12 sr","0.060 sr","0.24 sr","0.48 sr"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $dA = 0.030$ m$^2$, $r = 0.50$ m.\nFormula: $d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$.\nSubstituting: $d\\Omega = \\frac{0.030}{(0.50)^2} = \\frac{0.030}{0.25}$.\nSo the solid angle = 0.12 sr. Option (2) divides by $r$ instead of $r^2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A star has mass $4.0 \\times 10^{30}$ kg and radius $1.0 \\times 10^{9}$ m. Treating it as a uniform sphere, what is its average mass density?","image":"","options":["$\\approx 955$ kg $m^{-3}$","$4.0 \\times 10^{3}$ kg $m^{-3}$","$\\approx 1273$ kg $m^{-3}$","$\\approx 119$ kg $m^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $M = 4.0 \\times 10^{30}$ kg, $R = 1.0 \\times 10^9$ m.\nFormula: $\\rho = \\frac{M}{\\frac{4}{3}\\pi R^3}$.\nSubstituting: $V = 4.19 \\times 10^{27}$ $m^3$, so $\\rho = \\frac{4.0 \\times 10^{30}}{4.19 \\times 10^{27}} \\approx 955$ kg $m^{-3}$.\nThis is in the range of solid and liquid densities, not gases, even though a hot star is plasma.\nOption (2) omits $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi$. Option (4) wrongly uses the diameter as the radius.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A computed result is 3.865 s. According to the NCERT convention for rounding off uncertain digits, what is this value rounded to three significant figures?","image":"","options":["3.86 s","3.87 s","3.9 s","3.85 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rule: If the digit to be dropped is exactly 5, look at the digit before it.\nIf that preceding digit is even, simply drop the 5. If it is odd, raise the preceding digit by 1.\nHere the digit dropped is 5 and the preceding digit is 6, which is even. So the 5 is dropped and 3.865 becomes 3.86 s.\nThe tempting answer 3.87 s would come from always rounding 5 upward, which is not the NCERT convention. 3.9 s keeps only two significant figures.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In a new system of units, the unit of mass is 1 g, the unit of length is 1 cm and the unit of time is 1 minute. What is the numerical value of a power of 1 W in this system?","image":"","options":["$2.16 \\times 10^{12}$","$1.0 \\times 10^{7}$","$3.6 \\times 10^{10}$","$4.63 \\times 10^{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 1 W = 1 kg m$^2$ s$^{-3}$, with new units g, cm and min.\nConversions: 1 kg = $10^3$ g, 1 m$^2$ = $10^4$ cm$^2$, and 1 s = $\\frac{1}{60}$ min, so s$^{-3}$ = $60^3$ min$^{-3}$.\nSubstituting: $n = 10^3 \\times 10^4 \\times 60^3 = 10^7 \\times 2.16 \\times 10^5$.\nSo 1 W = $2.16 \\times 10^{12}$ new units. Option (2) ignores the change in the time unit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following numbers become 7.36 when rounded off to three significant figures using the NCERT convention?\n7.355, 7.365, 7.364, 7.357, 7.345, 7.366","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"7.355: drop 5, preceding 5 is odd, so raise it → 7.36 ✓\n7.365: drop 5, preceding 6 is even, so leave it → 7.36 ✓\n7.364: dropped 4 < 5 → 7.36 ✓\n7.357: dropped 7 > 5 → 7.36 ✓\n7.345: drop 5, preceding 4 is even → 7.34 ✗\n7.366: dropped 6 > 5 → 7.37 ✗\nFour numbers give 7.36.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A screw gauge of pitch 1 mm with 100 circular scale divisions measures length more precisely than vernier callipers whose 20 vernier divisions equal 19 main scale divisions of 1 mm each.\n(ii) Masses of 1.02 g and 9.89 g, each measured to $\\pm 0.01$ g, have nearly equal relative errors.\n(iii) Writing 2.308 cm as 23.08 mm changes the number of significant figures in the measurement.\n(iv) If $l = 16.2 \\pm 0.1$ cm and $b = 10.1 \\pm 0.1$ cm, the area is properly quoted as $164 \\pm 3$ $\\text{cm}^2$.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the screw gauge's least count is 1 mm/100 = 0.01 mm. The vernier's least count is 1 mm − 19/20 mm = 0.05 mm. A smaller least count means greater precision.\n(ii) Incorrect: the relative error is about 1 % for 1.02 g but about 0.1 % for 9.89 g. Relative error depends on the size of the number.\n(iii) Incorrect: a change of units never changes the number of significant figures. Both have four.\n(iv) Correct: the errors 0.6 % and 1 % add to 1.6 %, which on 163.62 cm² is about 2.6 cm². This is quoted as 164 ± 3 cm².\nTwo statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The length and breadth of a rectangular plate are measured as $25.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm and $8.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm respectively. What is the percentage error in the calculated area of the plate?","image":"","options":["0.65 %","1.25 %","1.65 %","2.00 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $l = 25.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm and $b = 8.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm.\nFormula: for a product, the percentage errors add: $\\frac{\\Delta A}{A}\\times100 = \\frac{\\Delta l}{l}\\times100 + \\frac{\\Delta b}{b}\\times100$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{0.1}{25.0}\\times100 = 0.4\\%$ and $\\frac{0.1}{8.0}\\times100 = 1.25\\%$.\nSo the percentage error in area = 0.4 + 1.25 = 1.65 %.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of physical quantities does not have same dimensional formula?","image":"","options":["Work and torque.","Angular momentum and Planck’s constant.","Tension and surface tension.","Impulse and linear momentum."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Work and torque: both are force × distance, $[ML^2T^{-2}]$. Same.\n(2) Angular momentum and Planck's constant: both are $[ML^2T^{-1}]$. Same.\n(3) Tension is a force, $[MLT^{-2}]$. Surface tension is force per length, $[MT^{-2}]$. Different.\n(4) Impulse = change in momentum, both $[MLT^{-1}]$. Same.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2.6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The displacement of a wave is given by $y = A\\sin(kx)$, where $x$ is a distance and $A$ is the amplitude. The dimensional formula of $k$ is:","image":"","options":["$[L]$","$[L^{-1}]$","$[M^0 L^0 T^0]$","$[L T^{-1}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The argument of a trigonometric function must be dimensionless.\nSo $[k][x] = [M^0L^0T^0]$, which gives $[k] = [L^{-1}]$.\nThe tempting option (3) is the dimension of the whole argument $kx$, not of $k$ alone.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the revised SI, the definition of the kilogram depends on the definitions of the metre and the second.\nReason (R): The kilogram is defined by fixing the numerical value of the Planck constant $h$ in J s, and J s equals kg m$^2$ s$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $h$ is fixed when expressed in J s, and J s = kg m$^2$ s$^{-1}$.\nA is true: to extract the kilogram from this unit, the metre and the second must already be defined, through $c$ and $\\Delta\\nu_{Cs}$.\nThe presence of m and s in the unit of $h$ is exactly why the kilogram depends on them, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In a new system, force, length and time are chosen as base quantities. Their units are 100 N, 10 m and 100 s respectively. The unit of mass in this system, expressed in kilograms, is:","image":"","options":["$10^{3}$ kg","$10$ kg","$10^{5}$ kg","$10^{7}$ kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: unit of force = 100 N, unit of length = 10 m, unit of time = 100 s.\nFormula: from $[F] = [M L T^{-2}]$, mass $= \\frac{F\\,T^2}{L}$.\nSubstituting: unit of mass $= \\frac{100 \\times (100)^2}{10} = 10^5$ kg\nSo the new unit of mass is $10^5$ kg. Option (1) results from forgetting to square the time.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Expressing 2.308 cm as 23.08 mm changes the number of significant figures.\n(ii) In $s = 2\\pi r$, the factor 2 is exact and has an infinite number of significant figures.\n(iii) A mass of 1.02 g and a mass of 9.89 g, each accurate to ±0.01 g, have relative errors of about 1% and 0.1% respectively.\n(iv) The result of 12.9 g − 7.06 g should be reported as 5.84 g.\n(v) The arguments of trigonometric, logarithmic and exponential functions must be dimensionless.\n(vi) The diameter of the earth ($1.28\\times10^7$ m) is 17 orders of magnitude larger than that of a hydrogen atom ($1.06\\times10^{-10}$ m).","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: a change of units never changes the number of significant figures.\n(ii) Correct: exact factors have infinite significant figures.\n(iii) Correct: 0.01/1.02 ≈ 1% and 0.01/9.89 ≈ 0.1%.\n(iv) Incorrect: the result must keep one decimal place, so it is 5.8 g.\n(v) Correct: these function arguments must be dimensionless.\n(vi) Correct: the orders of magnitude are 7 and −10, a difference of 17.\nCorrect statements = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In the FPS (British) system, the base units of length, mass and time are, respectively:","image":"","options":["foot, pound, second","foot, gram, second","metre, pound, second","centimetre, pound, minute"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the FPS base units as foot (length), pound (mass) and second (time).\nOption (2) wrongly takes gram, which is the CGS unit of mass.\nOption (3) wrongly takes metre, which is the MKS unit of length.\nOption (4) is wrong because the second is the unit of time in all three systems.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose force (F), velocity (V) and time (T) are chosen as the base quantities instead of mass, length and time. What is the dimensional formula of mass in this new system?","image":"","options":["$[F V T^{-1}]$","$[F V^{-1} T]$","$[F V^{-1} T^{-1}]$","$[F^{-1} V T]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $F = ma$, which gives $m = F/a$.\nAcceleration = velocity/time, so $a = V/T$.\nThen $m = \\frac{F}{V/T} = F V^{-1} T$.\nCheck: $[MLT^{-2}] \\cdot [L^{-1}T] \\cdot [T] = [M]$, which is correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs correctly matches a system of units with its base unit of mass?","image":"","options":["CGS – kilogram","MKS – gram","FPS – pound","MKS – pound"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The base unit of mass is the gram in CGS, the kilogram in MKS and the pound in FPS.\nOption (1) is wrong because CGS uses the gram, not the kilogram.\nOption (2) is wrong because MKS uses the kilogram, not the gram.\nOption (4) is wrong because the pound belongs to FPS, not MKS.\nOnly FPS – pound is correctly matched.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A screw gauge has a pitch of 0.5 mm and 50 divisions on its circular scale. What is its least count?","image":"","options":["0.01 mm","0.1 mm","0.001 mm","0.05 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: pitch = 0.5 mm and number of circular scale divisions = 50.\nFormula: least count = $\\frac{\\text{pitch}}{\\text{number of circular divisions}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{0.5}{50} = 0.01$ mm.\nSo the least count is 0.01 mm. A smaller least count means higher precision.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The relative density of mercury is 13.6. What is its density in SI units?","image":"","options":["$1.36 \\times 10^{4}$ kg m$^{-3}$","$1.36 \\times 10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$","$1.36 \\times 10^{5}$ kg m$^{-3}$","13.6 kg m$^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: relative density = 13.6, density of water = 1 g cm$^{-3}$ = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$.\nFormula: density = relative density × density of water.\nSubstituting: $13.6 \\times 1000$ kg m$^{-3}$.\nSo density = $1.36 \\times 10^4$ kg m$^{-3}$, which is 13.6 g cm$^{-3}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Expressing a length of 2.308 cm in millimetres changes its number of significant figures.\n(ii) In $T = \\frac{t}{n}$, where $n$ is the number of oscillations counted, $n$ has an infinite number of significant figures.\n(iii) For masses 1.02 g and 9.89 g, each measured to ±0.01 g, the relative error of the first is about ten times that of the second.\n(iv) A length written as 4700 mm (no decimal point) is correctly read as having four significant figures.\n(v) In multi-step calculations, intermediate results should keep one digit more than the least precise measurement.\n(vi) The argument of a logarithmic or exponential function in a physical formula must be dimensionless.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: a change of units never changes the number of significant figures.\n(ii) Correct: n is an exact counted number, so it has infinite significant figures.\n(iii) Correct: the relative errors are about 1% and 0.1%, a ratio of about 10.\n(iv) Incorrect: trailing zeros without a decimal point are taken as not significant, so it reads as two figures. Scientific notation removes this ambiguity.\n(v) Correct: the extra digit avoids rounding errors building up.\n(vi) Correct: arguments of special functions must be dimensionless.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Using the rule for rounding off given in NCERT, what is 8.335 rounded off to three significant figures?","image":"","options":["8.33","8.34","8.30","8.4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The digit to be dropped is exactly 5, and the preceding digit is 3.\nSince 3 is odd, the preceding digit is raised by 1, so 3 becomes 4.\nThe result is 8.34.\n8.33 would be correct only if the preceding digit were even.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Following the NCERT convention for rounding off, which of the following numbers has been rounded off INCORRECTLY to three significant figures?","image":"","options":["2.745 → 2.74","6.335 → 6.34","8.465 → 8.47","4.7462 → 4.75"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rule: if the digit to be dropped is exactly 5, it is simply dropped when the preceding digit is even. If the preceding digit is odd, the preceding digit is raised by 1.\n(1) 2.745: preceding digit 4 is even, so 2.74. Correct.\n(2) 6.335: preceding digit 3 is odd, so 6.34. Correct.\n(3) 8.465: preceding digit 6 is even, so it should be 8.46, not 8.47. Incorrect.\n(4) 4.7462: the dropped part (62) is more than 5 in the first digit, so 4.75. Correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A distance written as 3500 m (no decimal point) and the same distance written as $3.500 \\times 10^{3}$ m are both read, by the standard rules, as having the same number of significant figures.\nStatement II: In scientific notation $a \\times 10^{b}$, all zeros appearing in the base number $a$ are significant, while the power of 10 is irrelevant to the count.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. In 3500 m there is no decimal point, so the trailing zeros are not significant and it is read as 2 significant figures.\nIn $3.500 \\times 10^{3}$ m the trailing zeros sit in the base number, so they are significant and it has 4 significant figures.\nStatement II is true. This is exactly why scientific notation removes the ambiguity about trailing zeros. The exponent b plays no role in the count.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are SI base units?\nmetre, newton, kelvin, joule, candela, mole, watt, ampere","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The base units in the list are metre, kelvin, candela, mole and ampere, which makes 5.\nNewton (kg m s$^{-2}$), joule (kg m$^2$ s$^{-2}$) and watt (kg m$^2$ s$^{-3}$) are derived units with special names.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A picture of a building occupies an area of 2.0 $cm^2$ on a slide. When the slide is projected, the building occupies 3.2 $m^2$ on the screen. What is the linear magnification of the projector-screen arrangement?","image":"","options":["$\\approx 126$","$1.6 \\times 10^{4}$","40","$\\approx 253$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: slide area = 2.0 $cm^2$ = $2.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ $m^2$; screen area = 3.2 $m^2$.\nFormula: areal magnification = (linear magnification)$^2$.\nSubstituting: areal magnification = $\\frac{3.2}{2.0 \\times 10^{-4}} = 1.6 \\times 10^4$.\nSo linear magnification = $\\sqrt{1.6 \\times 10^4} \\approx 126$.\nOption (2) is the areal magnification, not the linear one.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The mean length of an object is 5 cm. Which of the following measurements is most accurate?","image":"","options":["4.9 cm","4.805 cm","5.25 cm","5.4 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Accuracy is how close a measurement is to the true value. Here the true value is taken as the mean, 5 cm.\nDeviations: 4.9 cm is off by 0.1 cm. 4.805 cm is off by 0.195 cm. 5.25 cm is off by 0.25 cm. 5.4 cm is off by 0.4 cm.\n4.9 cm has the smallest deviation, so it is the most accurate.\nWhy not (2): 4.805 cm has more digits, making it more precise but less accurate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2.10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In a complex multi-step calculation, NCERT advises retaining one digit more than the number of significant digits in intermediate steps. What is the main purpose of this practice?","image":"","options":["To increase the precision of the original measurements","To avoid additional errors building up from repeated rounding off","To make the final answer have more significant figures than the data","To make the calculation dimensionally consistent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rounding at every intermediate step adds a small error each time, and these errors can accumulate.\nKeeping one extra digit in intermediate results prevents this. The answer is rounded to proper significant figures only at the end.\nIt cannot improve the precision of the measured data itself.\nThe final result still must not carry more significant figures than the data allow.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If every term of an equation has the same dimensions, the equation must be a correct physical relation.\nReason (R): The method of dimensions cannot determine dimensionless constants, such as the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in $K = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. A dimensionally consistent equation need not be exact. For example, $K = \\frac{3}{16}mv^2$ passes the dimensional test but is wrong.\nOnly a dimensionally inconsistent equation is proved wrong.\nR is true. Dimensionless constants have no dimensions, so dimensional analysis cannot fix them. This is why it cannot choose between (1/2)mv² and (3/16)mv².\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A force $F$ acting on a particle varies as $F = A \\sin(Bx) + C \\ln(Dt)$, where $x$ is a distance and $t$ is time. What are the dimensions of $\\frac{A}{BD}$?","image":"","options":["$[M T^{-3}]$","$[M L^2 T^{-3}]$","$[M L^2 T^{-1}]$","$[M T^{-1}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The arguments of sine and logarithm must be dimensionless.\nFrom $Bx$ dimensionless: $[B] = [L^{-1}]$. From $Dt$ dimensionless: $[D] = [T^{-1}]$.\nSine is a pure number, so $A$ has the dimensions of force: $[A] = [M L T^{-2}]$.\n$\\frac{A}{BD} = \\frac{[M L T^{-2}]}{[L^{-1}][T^{-1}]} = [M L^2 T^{-1}]$.\nOption (2) comes from computing $\\frac{AD}{B}$ by mistake.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following measured values have exactly four significant figures?\n(i) 0.02308 m\n(ii) $4.700 \\times 10^{3}$ mm\n(iii) 12300 cm (obtained by converting 123 m)\n(iv) 0.06900 kg\n(v) 3.500 s\n(vi) 0.0050 m","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Four: 2, 3, 0, 8. The leading zeros are not significant.\n(ii) Four: all digits of the base number in scientific notation count.\n(iii) Three: trailing zeros in a number without a decimal point are not significant. Changing units also cannot add figures to 123 m.\n(iv) Four: 6, 9, 0, 0. Trailing zeros after a decimal point count.\n(v) Four.\n(vi) Two: only 5 and the final 0.\nSo four values qualify.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The SI scheme of units and symbols was developed by the BIPM in 1971 and revised by the General Conference on Weights and Measures in November 2018.\nStatement II: The second is defined by fixing the numerical value of the unperturbed ground-state hyperfine transition frequency of the caesium-133 atom, expressed in Hz.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the BIPM developed SI in 1971, and the General Conference on Weights and Measures revised it in November 2018.\nStatement II is correct: the second is defined through $\\Delta\\nu_{Cs}$ of caesium-133, with the unit Hz = s$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following measured values have exactly four significant figures?\n0.06900 m, 3.500 s, $2.308 \\times 10^{3}$ g, 0.0230 kg, $4.70 \\times 10^{2}$ cm, 1.002 N, 5000 m","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"0.06900: leading zeros are not significant and trailing zeros after a decimal are, giving 6, 9, 0, 0. That is 4, so yes.\n3.500: 4 significant figures, yes. $2.308 \\times 10^{3}$: 4, yes.\n0.0230: 3, no. $4.70 \\times 10^{2}$: 3, no.\n1.002: the zeros lie between non-zero digits, giving 4, yes.\n5000: trailing zeros without a decimal point are not significant, giving 1, no.\nThe total is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In the relation $r = \\frac{d}{2}$, where r is the radius and d is the measured diameter of a wire, how many significant figures does the factor 2 have?","image":"","options":["One","Two","Same as the number of significant figures in d","An infinite number"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The factor 2 is neither a rounded number nor a measured value. It is an exact number.\nExact numbers have an infinite number of significant figures, so 2 can be written as 2.0, 2.00 or 2.0000 as needed.\nOption (3) is tempting, but only the measured quantity d limits the precision of the result. The factor 2 itself does not.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Measure of two quantities along with the precision of respective measuring instrument is\nA = 2.5 m s$^{-1}$ $\\pm$ 0.5 m s$^{-1}$\nB = 0.10 s $\\pm$ 0.01 s\nThe value of AB will be","image":"","options":["(0.25 $\\pm$ 0.08) m","(0.25 $\\pm$ 0.5) m","(0.25 $\\pm$ 0.05) m","(0.25 $\\pm$ 0.135) m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 2.5 \\pm 0.5$ m s$^{-1}$ and $B = 0.10 \\pm 0.01$ s.\nFormula: for a product, $\\frac{\\Delta(AB)}{AB} = \\frac{\\Delta A}{A} + \\frac{\\Delta B}{B}$\nSubstituting: $AB = 0.25$ m. The relative error is $\\frac{0.5}{2.5} + \\frac{0.01}{0.10} = 0.3$.\nSo $\\Delta(AB) = 0.3 \\times 0.25 = 0.075 \\approx 0.08$ m, and $AB = (0.25 \\pm 0.08)$ m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2.7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Refractive index of a medium has no dimensions.\nReason (R): Refractive index is the ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to the speed of light in the medium.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT lists refractive index among dimensionless quantities.\nR is true: refractive index is defined as (speed in vacuum)/(speed in medium).\nA ratio of two similar physical quantities has dimensions $[LT^{-1}]/[LT^{-1}]$, which equals a pure number.\nSo R directly explains why A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In mechanics, the method of dimensions cannot deduce a product-type relation if the quantity depends on more than three independent variables.\nStatement II: Dimensional analysis cannot deduce a relation in which the quantity is a sum of terms, such as $s = ut + \\frac{1}{2}at^2$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Mechanics has only three base dimensions, M, L and T, so they give only three equations. With more than three unknown exponents, the equations cannot be solved uniquely.\nStatement II is correct. The method assumes a single product of powers. It cannot generate an expression made of several added terms, and it cannot give the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ either.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The length and breadth of a rectangular plate are measured as $l = 20.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm and $b = 5.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm. Using the combination of errors rule for a product, what is the absolute error in the calculated area?","image":"","options":["0.2 $\\text{cm}^2$","0.5 $\\text{cm}^2$","1.0 $\\text{cm}^2$","2.5 $\\text{cm}^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: l = 20.0 ± 0.1 cm (0.5 %) and b = 5.0 ± 0.1 cm (2 %).\nFormula: for a product, the percentage errors add. So the error in A = lb is 0.5 % + 2 % = 2.5 %.\nSubstituting: A = 20.0 × 5.0 = 100 cm². The absolute error is 2.5 % of 100 cm², which is 2.5 cm².\nSo the absolute error in the area = 2.5 cm². The option 0.2 cm² comes from wrongly adding the absolute errors.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match each number in List-I with its value in List-II when rounded off to three significant figures, using the NCERT rounding convention.\nList-I:\n(A) 3.625\n(B) 3.635\n(C) 3.647\n(D) 3.6349\nList-II:\n(I) 3.62\n(II) 3.63\n(III) 3.64\n(IV) 3.65","image":"","options":["A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) 3.625: drop 5, preceding 2 is even → 3.62 (I)\n(B) 3.635: drop 5, preceding 3 is odd → 3.64 (III)\n(C) 3.647: dropped 7 > 5 → 3.65 (IV)\n(D) 3.6349: the first digit to be dropped is 4, which is less than 5 → 3.63 (II). The trailing 9 does not matter here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about arithmetic with significant figures is correct?","image":"","options":["In multiplication, the result keeps as many decimal places as the factor with the fewest decimal places.","In addition, the result keeps as many decimal places as the number with the fewest decimal places.","The factor 2 in $s = 2\\pi r$ limits the circumference to one significant figure.","In division, the result may have more significant figures than the least precise input, because division improves precision."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: multiplication and division follow the rule of fewest significant figures, not decimal places.\n(2) Correct: addition and subtraction follow the rule of decimal places.\n(3) Incorrect: 2 is an exact number and has infinitely many significant figures.\n(4) Incorrect: a calculated result can never be more precise than the data it comes from.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The sum of the measured masses 12.43 g, 3.1 g and 0.256 g should be reported as 15.79 g.\nStatement II: In addition or subtraction, the final result should retain as many decimal places as there are in the number with the least decimal places.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II: Correct. For addition and subtraction, the rule is based on decimal places, not on significant figures.\nStatement I: The arithmetic sum is 15.786 g. The least precise value, 3.1 g, has only one decimal place.\nSo the result must be rounded to 15.8 g, not 15.79 g. Statement I is incorrect.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The frequency $f$ of a stretched string is assumed to depend on its length $l$, the tension $F$ in it and its mass per unit length $\\mu$ as $f = k\\, l^a F^b \\mu^c$, where $k$ is a dimensionless constant. Using the method of dimensions, the relation is:","image":"","options":["$f = \\frac{k}{l}\\sqrt{\\frac{F}{\\mu}}$","$f = k\\, l\\sqrt{\\frac{F}{\\mu}}$","$f = \\frac{k}{l}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu}{F}}$","$f = \\frac{k}{l^2}\\sqrt{\\frac{F}{\\mu}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Dimensions: $[f] = [T^{-1}]$, $[F] = [MLT^{-2}]$, $[\\mu] = [ML^{-1}]$, $[l] = [L]$.\nWriting the equation: $[M^0L^0T^{-1}] = [L]^a[MLT^{-2}]^b[ML^{-1}]^c$.\nEquating powers: M gives $b + c = 0$; T gives $-2b = -1$, so $b = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $c = -\\frac{1}{2}$.\nL gives $a + b - c = 0$, so $a = -1$.\nSo $f = \\frac{k}{l}\\sqrt{\\frac{F}{\\mu}}$. The value of $k$ cannot be found by this method.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A metal cube has mass $m = 40.0 \\pm 0.2$ g and side $a = 2.00 \\pm 0.01$ cm. Its density is $\\rho = \\frac{m}{a^3}$. What is the maximum absolute error in the calculated density?","image":"","options":["0.10 g cm$^{-3}$","0.05 g cm$^{-3}$","0.025 g cm$^{-3}$","0.20 g cm$^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 40.0 \\pm 0.2$ g and $a = 2.00 \\pm 0.01$ cm, so $\\rho = \\frac{40.0}{8.00} = 5.00$ g cm$^{-3}$.\nFormula: $\\frac{\\Delta\\rho}{\\rho} = \\frac{\\Delta m}{m} + 3\\frac{\\Delta a}{a}$. Relative errors add, and the cube power triples the side's error.\nSubstituting: 0.5 % + 3(0.5 %) = 2.0 %.\n$\\Delta\\rho = 0.02 \\times 5.00 = 0.10$ g cm$^{-3}$.\nThe option 0.05 comes from forgetting the factor 3.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The length of a rod is measured with a metre scale as $l = 25.0 \\pm 0.1$ cm. What is the percentage error in this measurement?","image":"","options":["0.1 %","0.4 %","4 %","2.5 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $l = 25.0$ cm and absolute error $\\Delta l = 0.1$ cm.\nFormula: percentage error $= \\frac{\\Delta l}{l} \\times 100$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{0.1}{25.0} \\times 100 = 0.4$.\nSo the percentage error is 0.4 %. The option 0.1 % wrongly treats the absolute error as the percentage error.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A student finds the difference of two measured masses, 15.8 g and 9.27 g. How should the result be correctly reported?","image":"","options":["6.53 g","6.5 g","7 g","6.530 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rule: in addition or subtraction, the result keeps as many decimal places as the number with the fewest decimal places.\nStep 1: Arithmetic difference = 15.8 − 9.27 = 6.53 g.\nStep 2: 15.8 g has only one decimal place, so round to one decimal place: 6.53 → 6.5 g.\nOption 6.53 g wrongly keeps the precision of 9.27 g. Option 7 g discards too much precision.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In Fig. 1.1(b) of NCERT, a cone with apex O intercepts an area $dA$ on a spherical surface of radius $r$ drawn with O as centre. The solid angle $d\\Omega$ and its SI unit are:","image":"","options":["$d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r}$, radian","$d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$, steradian","$d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$, radian","$d\\Omega = \\frac{ds}{r}$, steradian"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Solid angle is defined as the intercepted area divided by the square of the radius: $d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$.\nIts unit is the steradian (sr). It is dimensionless because it is area divided by area.\nThe radian (rad) and the ratio $\\frac{ds}{r}$ (arc length over radius) belong to the plane angle, not the solid angle.\n$\\frac{dA}{r}$ has the dimension of length, so it cannot be an angle.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In a new system of units, the unit of force is 100 N, the unit of length is 10 m and the unit of time is 100 s. The unit of mass in this system, expressed in kilograms, is:","image":"","options":["$10^{3}$ kg","$10^{4}$ kg","$10^{5}$ kg","$10^{6}$ kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: unit of force = 100 N, unit of length = 10 m, unit of time = 100 s.\nFormula: $[F] = [MLT^{-2}]$, so $M = \\frac{F\\,T^2}{L}$.\nSubstituting: $M = \\frac{100 \\times (100)^2}{10}$ kg $= \\frac{10^6}{10}$ kg.\nSo the unit of mass = $10^5$ kg.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many significant figures are there in the measured value 0.003050 kg?","image":"","options":["3","4","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The value is less than 1, so the zeros after the decimal point that come before the first non-zero digit (0.00) are not significant.\nThe zero between 3 and 5 is significant because it lies between two non-zero digits.\nThe trailing zero after 5 is significant because the number has a decimal point.\nThe significant digits are therefore 3, 0, 5, 0, which is four in all.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves along a circular arc of radius 2.0 m and covers an arc length of 0.50 m. What angle does the arc subtend at the centre?","image":"","options":["0.25 rad","4.0 rad","1.0 rad","0.50 rad"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: arc length $ds = 0.50$ m, radius $r = 2.0$ m.\nFormula: plane angle $d\\theta = \\frac{ds}{r}$.\nSubstituting: $d\\theta = \\frac{0.50}{2.0}$.\nSo the angle = 0.25 rad. Option (2) wrongly uses $r/ds$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The length and breadth of a rectangular sheet are 16.2 cm and 10.1 cm, respectively. The area of the sheet in appropriate significant figures and error is","image":"","options":["164 $\\pm$ 3 cm$^{2}$","163.62 $\\pm$ 2.6 cm$^{2}$","163.6 $\\pm$ 2.6 cm$^{2}$","163.62 $\\pm$ 3 cm$^{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $l = 16.2$ cm and $b = 10.1$ cm. Take an uncertainty of $\\pm 0.1$ cm in each, from the last decimal place.\nFormula: $\\frac{\\Delta A}{A} = \\frac{\\Delta l}{l} + \\frac{\\Delta b}{b}$\nSubstituting: $A = 163.62$ cm$^2$ and $\\frac{\\Delta A}{A} = \\frac{0.1}{16.2} + \\frac{0.1}{10.1} \\approx 0.016$. So $\\Delta A \\approx 2.6$ cm$^2$.\nThe data have 3 significant figures, so the area is rounded to 3 significant figures. The error is rounded to match.\nSo $A = 164 \\pm 3$ cm$^2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2.5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The result of subtracting 4.08 g from 15.6 g should be reported as 11.5 g and not as 11.52 g.\nStatement II: In addition or subtraction, the result should retain as many significant figures as the number with the least significant figures.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The arithmetic gives 15.6 − 4.08 = 11.52. The least precise value, 15.6, has one decimal place, so the result is 11.5 g.\nStatement II is incorrect. For addition and subtraction the rule is based on decimal places, not on significant figures. The significant-figure rule applies to multiplication and division.\nHere both rules happen to give 11.5, but Statement II is wrong as a general rule.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Two lengths measured with the same scale are 0.508 m and 0.505 m.\nAssertion (A): Their difference should be reported as $3 \\times 10^{-3}$ m and not as $3.00 \\times 10^{-3}$ m.\nReason (R): In subtraction, the result keeps as many decimal places as the term with the least decimal places, not as many significant figures as the data.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: for addition and subtraction, NCERT applies the rule in terms of decimal places.\nA is true: 0.508 − 0.505 = 0.003 m, with 3 decimal places. This equals $3 \\times 10^{-3}$ m, which has only one significant figure.\nWriting $3.00 \\times 10^{-3}$ m would wrongly apply the multiplication/division rule (3 significant figures) and overstate the precision. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"According to the principle of homogeneity of dimensions, which of the following pairs of quantities can be meaningfully added to each other?","image":"","options":["Velocity and force","Kinetic energy and potential energy","Electric current and thermodynamic temperature","Mass and linear momentum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only quantities with the same dimensions can be added or subtracted.\nKinetic energy and potential energy both have dimensions $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$, so they can be added.\nVelocity $[L T^{-1}]$ and force $[M L T^{-2}]$ have different dimensions. Current [A] and temperature [K] are different base quantities.\nMass [M] and momentum $[M L T^{-1}]$ also differ, so (4) is the tempting but wrong choice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A change in velocity and an average speed have the same dimensional formula, $[M^0 L T^{-1}]$.\nStatement II: The dimensional formula of a physical quantity also tells us its magnitude.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Change in velocity, initial velocity, average velocity and speed can all be written as length/time, so each has dimensions $[L T^{-1}]$.\nStatement II is incorrect. Dimensions describe only the type (nature) of a quantity. Magnitudes are not considered in this representation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In the revised SI, which base unit is defined by fixing the numerical value of the Planck constant $h$, expressed in the unit J s ($= \\text{kg m}^2\\text{ s}^{-1}$)?","image":"","options":["metre","kilogram","kelvin","ampere"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The kilogram is defined by fixing the numerical value of the Planck constant $h$ in the unit J s, which equals kg m$^2$ s$^{-1}$.\nThe metre and second in that unit are already defined through $c$ and the caesium frequency $\\Delta\\nu_{Cs}$. Only the kilogram is left to be fixed by $h$.\nThe metre is defined through $c$, not $h$.\nThe kelvin is defined through the Boltzmann constant $k$.\nThe ampere is defined through the elementary charge $e$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"According to the NCERT convention for rounding off, what does 3.465 become when rounded to three significant figures?","image":"","options":["3.47","3.46","3.45","3.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The digit to be dropped is exactly 5, and the preceding digit is 6.\nNCERT convention: if the preceding digit is even, the 5 is simply dropped. If it is odd, the preceding digit is raised by 1.\nSince 6 is even, the 5 is dropped and the result is 3.46.\n3.47 is the tempting choice from the 'always round 5 up' habit, but it is wrong under this convention.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match each SI base unit in List-I with the fundamental constant whose fixed numerical value is used to define it in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) metre\n(B) kilogram\n(C) ampere\n(D) kelvin\nList-II:\n(I) Planck constant $h$\n(II) Boltzmann constant $k$\n(III) speed of light in vacuum $c$\n(IV) elementary charge $e$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Metre: fixed value of $c$ in m s$^{-1}$, so A-III.\nKilogram: fixed value of $h$ in J s, so B-I.\nAmpere: fixed value of $e$ in coulomb (A s), so C-IV.\nKelvin: fixed value of $k$ in J K$^{-1}$, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the dimensions of force is correct?","image":"","options":["Force has one dimension in mass, one in length and –2 in time.","Force has one dimension in mass, two in length and –2 in time.","Force has one dimension in mass, one in length and –1 in time.","Force has zero dimension in mass, one in length and –2 in time."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Force = mass × acceleration = mass × length/(time)$^2$.\nSo $[F] = [M L T^{-2}]$.\nThe exponents are 1 in M, 1 in L and –2 in T.\nOption (2) gives the dimensions of energy, (3) those of momentum and (4) those of acceleration.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A grocer's balance shows the mass of a packet as 4.6 kg. Two silver coins of masses 12.35 g and 12.38 g, measured on a precise balance, are placed in the packet.\nStatement I: The total mass of the packet with the coins should be reported as 4.6 kg.\nStatement II: The difference in the masses of the two coins should be reported as 0.030 g.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The total is 4.6 + 0.01235 + 0.01238 = 4.62473 kg. The least precise term (4.6 kg) has 1 decimal place, so the sum rounds to 4.6 kg.\nStatement II is incorrect. The difference is 12.38 − 12.35 = 0.03 g. Both masses have 2 decimal places, so the result must also have 2 decimal places: 0.03 g, not 0.030 g. The extra zero claims a precision that the data do not have.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If measured data are given to n significant figures, a result obtained by combining them is generally valid to n significant figures, but subtraction of such data can reduce the number of significant figures.\nStatement II: Two measurements having the same absolute uncertainty always have the same relative error.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that combined results are valid to n significant figures, but subtraction can reduce this number. For example, 12.9 g − 7.06 g is reported as 5.8 g (two significant figures).\nStatement II is incorrect. Relative error = absolute error ÷ value, so it also depends on the measured value. For example, ±0.01 g on 1.02 g is about 1 %, while on 9.89 g it is about 0.1 %.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Each number below is rounded off to three significant figures using the NCERT convention. How many of the roundings are correct?\n(i) 2.745 → 2.74\n(ii) 2.735 → 2.73\n(iii) 2.746 → 2.75\n(iv) 4.365 → 4.36\n(v) 8.155 → 8.15","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rule: if the dropped digit is exactly 5, keep the preceding digit if it is even and raise it by 1 if it is odd.\n(i) Correct: 4 is even, so 2.745 → 2.74.\n(ii) Incorrect: 3 is odd, so the answer should be 2.74.\n(iii) Correct: the dropped digit 6 is more than 5, so round up to 2.75.\n(iv) Correct: 6 is even, so 4.365 → 4.36.\n(v) Incorrect: 5 is odd, so the answer should be 8.16.\nThree roundings are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The numbers 2.745 and 2.735 on rounding off to 3 significant figures will give","image":"","options":["2.75 and 2.74","2.74 and 2.73","2.75 and 2.73","2.74 and 2.74"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT rounding rule: if the dropped digit is exactly 5, leave the preceding digit unchanged when it is even. Raise it by one when it is odd.\n2.745: the preceding digit 4 is even, so it gives 2.74.\n2.735: the preceding digit 3 is odd, so it becomes 4, giving 2.74.\nBoth round to 2.74. Option (1) wrongly always rounds 5 upward.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2.4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The pressure $P$ in a certain system varies as $P = \\frac{a - t^2}{b\\,x}$, where $t$ is time, $x$ is a distance, and $a$, $b$ are constants. The dimensional formula of $\\frac{a}{b}$ is:","image":"","options":["$[M T^{-2}]$","$[M^{-1} T^{2}]$","$[M L T^{-2}]$","$[M T^{-4}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Since $a$ is subtracted from $t^2$, $[a] = [T^2]$.\nFrom the equation, $[b] = [T^2]/([P][x])$. With $[P] = [ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$ and $[x] = [L]$, the product $[P][x] = [MT^{-2}]$.\nSo $[b] = [T^2]/[MT^{-2}] = [M^{-1}T^4]$.\nThen $[a/b] = [T^2]/[M^{-1}T^4] = [MT^{-2}]$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The acceleration due to gravity is 10 m s$^{-2}$. Its value in a system where the unit of length is 1 km and the unit of time is 1 minute is:","image":"","options":["0.6","36","600","2160"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $n_1 = 10$, $L_1/L_2 = \\frac{1}{1000}$, $T_1/T_2 = \\frac{1}{60}$.\nFormula: $n_2 = n_1\\left(\\frac{L_1}{L_2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{T_1}{T_2}\\right)^{-2}$, since $[g] = [L T^{-2}]$.\nSubstituting: $n_2 = 10 \\times \\frac{1}{1000} \\times 3600 = 36$\nSo g = 36 km min$^{-2}$. Option (1), 0.6, results from using the time factor only once instead of squared.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The dimensional formula of mass density is written in terms of $[M]$, $[L]$ and $[T]$. What is the dimension of mass density in length?","image":"","options":["$+3$","$-3$","$-1$","$0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mass density = mass / volume.\nVolume is the product of three lengths, so $[V] = [L^3]$.\nSo $[\\rho] = [M]/[L^3] = [M L^{-3} T^0]$.\nThe exponent of $[L]$ is $-3$. Option (1) gives the sign of volume's exponent, not density's.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of measured values have the SAME number of significant figures?","image":"","options":["0.0500 kg and 500 kg","$3.20 \\times 10^{-5}$ m and 320.0 m","0.07010 s and $7.010 \\times 10^{4}$ s","1.00 N and 100 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) 0.0500 has 3 significant figures (5, 0, 0). 500 has no decimal point, so it has only 1. They differ.\n(2) $3.20 \\times 10^{-5}$ has 3. 320.0 has a decimal point, so all its digits count, giving 4. They differ.\n(3) 0.07010 has 4 (7, 0, 1, 0). $7.010 \\times 10^{4}$ also has 4, since the power of 10 is irrelevant. They match.\n(4) 1.00 has 3, but 100 has 1. They differ.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The steradian is the SI unit of solid angle. It is defined through $d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$, where $dA$ is the area intercepted on a sphere of radius $r$ centred at the apex.\nStatement II: The radian has the dimension [L], because it is defined using an arc length.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT defines the solid angle as intercepted area divided by $r^2$, and its unit is the steradian (sr).\nStatement II: Incorrect. The radian is a ratio, arc length divided by radius, which is [L]/[L]. So the plane angle is dimensionless.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match each quantity in MKS base units (List-I) with its value in CGS base units (List-II).\nList-I:\n(a) 1 kg m s$^{-2}$\n(b) 1 kg m$^{2}$ s$^{-2}$\n(c) 1 kg m$^{-3}$\n(d) 1 m s$^{-2}$\nList-II:\n(i) $10^{2}$ cm s$^{-2}$\n(ii) $10^{-3}$ g cm$^{-3}$\n(iii) $10^{5}$ g cm s$^{-2}$\n(iv) $10^{7}$ g cm$^{2}$ s$^{-2}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use 1 kg = $10^3$ g and 1 m = $10^2$ cm.\n(a) $10^3\\times10^2$ = $10^5$ g cm s$^{-2}$, so (iii).\n(b) $10^3\\times(10^2)^2$ = $10^7$ g cm$^2$ s$^{-2}$, so (iv).\n(c) $10^3\\times(10^2)^{-3}$ = $10^{-3}$ g cm$^{-3}$, so (ii).\n(d) 1 m s$^{-2}$ = $10^2$ cm s$^{-2}$, so (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Quantity)\n(A) Volume\n(B) Acceleration\n(C) Mass density\n(D) Force\nList-II (Dimensional formula)\n(I) $[M L T^{-2}]$\n(II) $[M^0 L^3 T^0]$\n(III) $[M L^{-3} T^0]$\n(IV) $[M^0 L T^{-2}]$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Volume = length × breadth × height, so it is $[M^0L^3T^0]$ (II).\n(B) Acceleration = length/time², so it is $[M^0LT^{-2}]$ (IV).\n(C) Mass density = mass/volume, so it is $[ML^{-3}T^0]$ (III).\n(D) Force = mass × acceleration, so it is $[MLT^{-2}]$ (I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A carton is weighed on a shop balance as 1.45 kg. Two silver coins of masses 35.62 g and 35.58 g, measured on a precise balance, are placed in it.\nStatement I: The total mass of the carton with the coins should be reported as 1.52 kg.\nStatement II: The difference in the masses of the two coins should be reported as 0.040 g, because each coin's mass has four significant figures.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: The coins add 0.07120 kg, so the total is 1.45 + 0.07120 = 1.5212 kg. The carton's mass is known only to 2 decimal places in kg, so the total is 1.52 kg. Correct.\nStatement II: 35.62 − 35.58 = 0.04 g. Subtraction keeps 2 decimal places, giving just one significant figure. Writing 0.040 g claims a precision the data do not have. Incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although there are a very large number of physical quantities, only a limited number of units is needed to express all of them.\nReason (R): Physical quantities are inter-related, so the units of derived quantities can be expressed as combinations of base units.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that a limited number of units can express all physical quantities.\nR is true: quantities are inter-related, and derived units are combinations of base units.\nR explains A: because derived units come from the few base units, a small set of units is enough.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The speed of light in vacuum, known to many significant figures as $2.99792458 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s, is often rounded off to $3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s in computations.\nStatement II: Using the NCERT convention, 0.0465 rounded off to two significant figures is 0.047.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT notes that c is commonly rounded off to $3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s for calculations.\nStatement II is incorrect. In 0.0465, the leading zeros are not significant, so the two significant figures are 4 and 6. The digit to be dropped is 5.\nThe preceding digit 6 is even, so the 5 is simply dropped. The result is 0.046, not 0.047.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An equation that is dimensionally consistent must be an exact (correct) physical equation.\nReason (R): The method of dimensions cannot give the values of dimensionless constants appearing in a relation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. Passing the dimensional test does not prove an equation correct.\nFor example, $K=\\frac{3}{16}mv^2$ is dimensionally correct, but it is not the right formula for kinetic energy.\nOnly a dimensionally inconsistent equation is certainly wrong.\nR is true. Dimensionless constants such as $\\frac{1}{2}$ or $2\\pi$ cannot be found by dimensional analysis.\nThis limitation is exactly why A fails.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"One calorie is about 4.2 J, where 1 J = 1 kg m$^2$ s$^{-2}$. In a new system, the unit of mass is 2 kg, the unit of length is 3 m and the unit of time is 4 s. What is the magnitude of one calorie in the new units?","image":"","options":["2.10","4.73","1.17","3.73"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\alpha = 2$, $\\beta = 3$, $\\gamma = 4$, and 1 cal = 4.2 J.\nFormula: magnitude $= 4.2\\,\\alpha^{-1}\\beta^{-2}\\gamma^{2}$, since energy has dimensions $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$.\nSubstituting: $4.2 \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{9} \\times 16 = 3.73$\nSo 1 calorie = 3.73 new units of energy.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The frequency $f$ of small oscillations of a liquid drop is assumed to depend only on the surface tension $S$ (dimensions $[M T^{-2}]$), the density $\\rho$ of the liquid and the radius $r$ of the drop. Using the method of dimensions, $f$ is proportional to:","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{\\frac{S}{\\rho r^3}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{\\rho r^3}{S}}$","$\\frac{S}{\\rho r^2}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{S r}{\\rho}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $f = k S^a \\rho^b r^c$.\nThen $[T^{-1}] = [M T^{-2}]^a [M L^{-3}]^b [L]^c$.\nEquating powers:\n- M: a + b = 0\n- L: −3b + c = 0\n- T: −2a = −1\nSo a = 1/2, b = −1/2 and c = 3b = −3/2.\nTherefore $f \\propto \\sqrt{\\frac{S}{\\rho r^3}}$.\nOption (2) has dimensions of time, not frequency, so it is the inverse of the answer.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A student views a fine fibre through a microscope of magnification 200. The average width of the fibre in the field of view is 7.0 mm. What is the actual thickness of the fibre?","image":"","options":["0.035 mm","0.35 mm","1400 mm","0.0035 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: magnification $m = 200$, observed width = 7.0 mm.\nFormula: actual size = observed size / magnification.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{7.0}{200} = 0.035$ mm.\nSo the thickness is 0.035 mm, i.e. $3.5 \\times 10^{-5}$ m. Multiplying instead of dividing gives the wrong value 1400 mm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (SI base quantity) with List-II (SI unit):\nList-I: (A) Amount of substance (B) Luminous intensity (C) Thermodynamic temperature (D) Electric current\nList-II: (I) kelvin (II) ampere (III) mole (IV) candela","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The amount of substance is measured in mole (mol), so A-III.\n(B) Luminous intensity is measured in candela (cd), so B-IV.\n(C) Thermodynamic temperature is measured in kelvin (K), so C-I.\n(D) Electric current is measured in ampere (A), so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The radius of the earth is $6.4 \\times 10^{6}$ m. What is its order of magnitude?","image":"","options":["6","7","5","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Here $a = 6.4$ and $b = 6$.\nSince $5 < 6.4 \\le 10$, $a$ is rounded to 10.\nSo $6.4 \\times 10^6 \\approx 10 \\times 10^6 = 10^7$ m.\nThe order of magnitude is 7, not 6. Option (1) wrongly reads the exponent directly without rounding $a$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Using the NCERT convention for order of magnitude (write the number as $a \\times 10^b$; round $a$ to 1 if $a \\le 5$ and to 10 if $5 < a \\le 10$):\nStatement I: The diameter of the earth ($1.28 \\times 10^7$ m) is 17 orders of magnitude larger than the diameter of a hydrogen atom ($1.06 \\times 10^{-10}$ m).\nStatement II: A length of $7.5 \\times 10^{-4}$ m has order of magnitude $-4$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. 1.28 ≤ 5, so the earth's diameter is of order $10^7$ m. 1.06 ≤ 5, so the atom's diameter is of order $10^{-10}$ m. The difference is 7 − (−10) = 17.\nStatement II: Incorrect. Here a = 7.5 > 5, so it rounds to 10. Then $10 \\times 10^{-4} = 10^{-3}$, giving order of magnitude −3, not −4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A student remembers the relativistic kinetic energy of a particle of rest mass $m_0$ moving with speed $v$, but forgets every factor of the speed of light $c$. He writes $K = m_0\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - v^2}} - 1\\right)$. Using only dimensional consistency, which of the following restored forms is acceptable?","image":"","options":["$K = m_0 c^2\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - v^2}} - 1\\right)$","$K = m_0 c^2\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{v^2}{c^2}}} - 1\\right)$","$K = m_0 c\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{v^2}{c^2}}} - 1\\right)$","$K = \\frac{m_0}{c^2}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{v^2}{c^2}}} - 1\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Two conditions must hold.\n(a) The bracket subtracts $v^2$ from the pure number 1, so $v^2$ must become dimensionless, as $\\frac{v^2}{c^2}$. This rules out option (1).\n(b) With the bracket now dimensionless, the prefactor must carry energy dimensions $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$. That requires $m_0 c^2$.\n$m_0 c$ has dimensions $[M L T^{-1}]$, and $\\frac{m_0}{c^2}$ has $[M L^{-2} T^2]$, so options (3) and (4) fail.\nOnly option (2) satisfies both conditions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A force of 5 N (5 kg m s$^{-2}$) is to be expressed in CGS base units. Its value in g cm s$^{-2}$ is:","image":"","options":["$5\\times10^{2}$ g cm s$^{-2}$","$5\\times10^{3}$ g cm s$^{-2}$","$5\\times10^{5}$ g cm s$^{-2}$","$5\\times10^{7}$ g cm s$^{-2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: F = 5 kg m s$^{-2}$, with 1 kg = $10^3$ g and 1 m = $10^2$ cm.\nFormula: Treat the units as algebraic quantities and convert each one.\nSubstituting: F = 5 × ($10^3$ g) × ($10^2$ cm) s$^{-2}$.\nSo F = $5\\times10^{5}$ g cm s$^{-2}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (unit) with List-II (expression in SI base units).\nList-I:\n(a) newton\n(b) joule\n(c) watt\n(d) radian\nList-II:\n(i) kg m$^2$ s$^{-3}$\n(ii) m m$^{-1}$\n(iii) kg m s$^{-2}$\n(iv) kg m$^2$ s$^{-2}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Newton: force = mass × acceleration, giving kg m s$^{-2}$, which is (iii).\n(b) Joule: work = force × distance, giving kg m$^2$ s$^{-2}$, which is (iv).\n(c) Watt: power = energy/time, giving kg m$^2$ s$^{-3}$, which is (i).\n(d) Radian: arc/radius, giving m m$^{-1}$ (dimensionless), which is (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following units lies outside SI but is retained for general use?","image":"","options":["newton","kelvin","litre","steradian"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The litre is listed among the units retained for general use even though it lies outside SI.\nThe newton is an SI derived unit with a special name.\nThe kelvin is an SI base unit.\nThe steradian is the SI unit of solid angle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In the relation $s = 2\\pi r$ for the circumference of a circle, the factor 2 should be regarded as having","image":"","options":["one significant figure","two significant figures, since it can be written as 2.0","an infinite number of significant figures","as many significant figures as the measured radius r"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The factor 2 in $s = 2\\pi r$ is neither a rounded number nor a measured value. It is an exact number.\nExact numbers have an infinite number of significant figures. The 2 can be written as 2.0, 2.00 or 2.0000 as needed.\nBecause of this, it never limits the significant figures of the result. Only the measured value of r does.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A student writes the kinematic relation as $v^2 = u^2 + 4as$. This relation passes the test of dimensional consistency.\nStatement II: The method of dimensions cannot determine dimensionless constants, so a dimensionally correct equation need not be an exact equation.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: $[v^2] = [u^2] = [L^2 T^{-2}]$ and $[4as] = [L T^{-2}][L] = [L^2 T^{-2}]$. The equation is homogeneous, so it passes the test. Correct.\nThe correct relation has the factor 2, not 4. Dimensional analysis cannot detect this error because numbers have no dimensions.\nStatement II: This is the NCERT conclusion. A dimensionally wrong equation must be wrong, but a dimensionally correct one is not proved right. Correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The equation $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = mgh$ is dimensionally correct.\nReason (R): Both sides of this equation have the dimensions $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The LHS has dimensions $[M][LT^{-1}]^2 = [ML^2T^{-2}]$. The factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ is dimensionless.\nThe RHS has dimensions $[M][LT^{-2}][L] = [ML^2T^{-2}]$.\nThe two sides match, so A is true.\nR states exactly this matching, which is why A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A new system of units takes the unit of mass as 1 g, the unit of length as 1 cm and the unit of time as 1 ms (millisecond). What is the numerical value of a power of 1 W in this system?","image":"","options":["$10^{-2}$","$10^{7}$","$10^{-5}$","$10^{16}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 1 kg = $10^3$ g, 1 m = $10^2$ cm, 1 s = $10^3$ ms.\nFormula: 1 W = 1 kg m$^2$ s$^{-3}$.\nSubstituting: 1 W = $(10^3\\,\\text{g})(10^2\\,\\text{cm})^2(10^3\\,\\text{ms})^{-3}$.\nThis gives $10^{3} \\times 10^{4} \\times 10^{-9}$ g cm$^2$ ms$^{-3}$.\nSo 1 W = $10^{-2}$ new units of power.\nOption (4), $10^{16}$, comes from wrongly writing s$^{-3}$ as $(10^3)^{+3}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following quantities are dimensionless?\nPlane angle, solid angle, strain, refractive index, relative density, angular velocity, momentum","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plane angle = arc/radius: dimensionless.\nSolid angle = area/radius$^2$: dimensionless.\nStrain = change in length/original length: dimensionless. Refractive index is a ratio of speeds, and relative density is a ratio of densities, so both are dimensionless.\nAngular velocity is $[T^{-1}]$ and momentum is $[MLT^{-1}]$, so neither is dimensionless.\nThat gives 5 dimensionless quantities.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match each calculation in List-I with its correctly reported result in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) 4.0 × 2.115\n(B) 14.27 − 6.1\n(C) 18.6 ÷ 3.00\n(D) 2.03 + 0.0041\nList-II\n(I) 8.2\n(II) 8.5\n(III) 6.20\n(IV) 2.03","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) 4.0 × 2.115 = 8.46. Keep 2 significant figures (from 4.0): 8.5, which is II.\n(B) 14.27 − 6.1 = 8.17. Keep 1 decimal place (from 6.1): 8.2, which is I.\n(C) 18.6 ÷ 3.00 = 6.2. Both inputs have 3 significant figures, so report 6.20, which is III.\n(D) 2.03 + 0.0041 = 2.0341. Keep 2 decimal places (from 2.03): 2.03, which is IV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A student reports the period of oscillation of a simple pendulum as 1.62 s. Which of the following correctly describes the digits in this reported value?","image":"","options":["Digits 1 and 6 are reliable and the digit 2 is the first uncertain digit, so there are three significant figures","All three digits 1, 6 and 2 are certain, so there are three significant figures","Only the digit 1 is reliable, while 6 and 2 are both uncertain","Only the digits 1 and 6 count as significant, because uncertain digits are never included"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A reported measurement contains all the reliably known digits plus the first uncertain digit. Together these are the significant figures.\nIn 1.62 s, the digits 1 and 6 are reliable and the last digit 2 is uncertain.\nSo the value has three significant figures.\nOption (4) is wrong because the first uncertain digit is always counted as significant.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following unit relations are correct?\n(i) 1 nm = 10 Å\n(ii) 1 fm = $10^{-12}$ m\n(iii) 1 GW = $10^{9}$ W\n(iv) 1 kW h = $3.6 \\times 10^{6}$ J\n(v) 1 light year ≈ $9.46 \\times 10^{15}$ m\n(vi) 1 Å = $10^{-8}$ m","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $10^{-9}$ m = $10 \\times 10^{-10}$ m = 10 Å.\n(ii) Incorrect: femto means $10^{-15}$, so 1 fm = $10^{-15}$ m.\n(iii) Correct: giga means $10^9$.\n(iv) Correct: $10^3$ W × 3600 s = $3.6 \\times 10^6$ J.\n(v) Correct: $3 \\times 10^8$ m s$^{-1}$ × $3.156 \\times 10^7$ s ≈ $9.46 \\times 10^{15}$ m.\n(vi) Incorrect: 1 Å = $10^{-10}$ m, which equals $10^{-8}$ cm.\nSo four relations are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Using $n_1u_1 = n_2u_2$ and $[F] = [M L T^{-2}]$, a force of 1 N expressed in CGS units (dyne) is:","image":"","options":["$10^{2}$ dyne","$10^{3}$ dyne","$10^{5}$ dyne","$10^{7}$ dyne"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $n_1 = 1$ in SI units (kg, m, s). The CGS units are g, cm, s.\nFormula: $n_2 = n_1\\left(\\frac{M_1}{M_2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{L_1}{L_2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{T_1}{T_2}\\right)^{-2}$\nSubstituting: $n_2 = 1 \\times 1000 \\times 100 \\times 1$\nSo 1 N = $10^5$ dyne. Option (4), $10^7$, is the factor for the joule, where length enters squared.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a limitation of the method of dimensions?","image":"","options":["It cannot give the values of dimensionless constants","It cannot distinguish between physical quantities that have the same dimensions","It cannot check whether the two sides of an equation are dimensionally consistent","It cannot by itself establish that a dimensionally correct equation is exactly correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Is a limitation: constants such as $2\\pi$ cannot be found.\n(2) Is a limitation: for example, work and torque have the same dimensions and cannot be told apart.\n(3) Is NOT a limitation: checking dimensional consistency is one of the main uses of the method.\n(4) Is a limitation: a dimensionally correct equation passes the test but is not thereby proved correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A student finds $\\frac{1}{9.58}$ and rounds it to three significant figures. He then takes the reciprocal of this rounded value, again to three significant figures. What value does he get?","image":"","options":["9.58","9.62","9.60","9.61"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: $\\frac{1}{9.58}$ = 0.10438..., which rounds to 0.104 at three significant figures.\nStep 2: $\\frac{1}{0.104}$ = 9.615..., which rounds to 9.62 at three significant figures.\nThe original 9.58 is not recovered because of the premature rounding.\nIf one extra digit had been kept (0.1044), then $\\frac{1}{0.1044}$ = 9.578 → 9.58.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The mass of a beaker filled with a liquid is 58.36 g, and the mass of the empty beaker, measured on a different balance, is 52.1 g. The volume of the liquid is 2.48 $cm^3$. What is the density of the liquid, reported to correct significant figures?","image":"","options":["2.524 g $cm^{-3}$","2.52 g $cm^{-3}$","2.5 g $cm^{-3}$","2.54 g $cm^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: Find the mass of the liquid by subtraction. m = 58.36 − 52.1 = 6.26 g. The result is limited to 1 decimal place, so m = 6.3 g, which has only 2 significant figures.\nStep 2: Find the density by division. $\\rho = \\frac{6.26}{2.48}$ = 2.524 g $cm^{-3}$ (one extra digit is kept in the intermediate step).\nStep 3: The mass has 2 significant figures, so the density is reported as 2.5 g $cm^{-3}$.\nThe tempting answer 2.52 g $cm^{-3}$ ignores the loss of significant figures in the subtraction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The equation $x = x_0 + v_0t + at^2$ (written without the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$) is dimensionally consistent.\nStatement II: The method of dimensions cannot determine the values of dimensionless constants in an equation.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: $[x] = [x_0] = [L]$. Also $[v_0t] = [LT^{-1}][T] = [L]$ and $[at^2] = [LT^{-2}][T^2] = [L]$. Every term is a length, so the equation is consistent, even though it is physically wrong. Correct.\nStatement II: numbers like $\\frac{1}{2}$ have no dimensions, so the method cannot fix them. Correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following measured values have exactly three significant figures?\n(i) 0.0230 m\n(ii) $2.30 \\times 10^{4}$ kg\n(iii) 2300 s (written without a decimal point)\n(iv) 230.0 g\n(v) 0.203 N\n(vi) 3.00 J","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) The leading zeros are not significant, so 2, 3, 0 are counted: 3 significant figures.\n(ii) Every digit of the base number 2.30 is significant: 3 significant figures.\n(iii) The trailing zeros have no decimal point, so they are not significant: 2 significant figures.\n(iv) The trailing zero comes after a decimal point, so it counts: 4 significant figures.\n(v) The zero between non-zero digits counts, but the leading zero does not: 3 significant figures.\n(vi) The trailing zeros come after a decimal point: 3 significant figures.\nValues (i), (ii), (v) and (vi) qualify, so the count is 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The relation $x = x_0 + v_0 t + \\frac{1}{2} a t^2$ for a body moving with uniform acceleration is dimensionally consistent.\nReason (R): Each term on the right-hand side has the dimension [L], which is the same as the dimension of $x$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Check each term. $[x_0] = [L]$.\n$[v_0 t] = [L T^{-1}][T] = [L]$.\n$[\\frac{1}{2} a t^2] = [L T^{-2}][T^2] = [L]$, because the factor 1/2 is dimensionless.\nAll the terms match the dimension of the left-hand side, [L]. So A is true, and R is exactly the reason A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The displacement of a particle varies with time as $x = A\\sin(Bt) + Ct^2$, where $x$ is in metres and $t$ is in seconds. What are the dimensions of $\\frac{AC}{B}$?","image":"","options":["$[L^2 T^{-3}]$","$[L T^{-1}]$","$[L^2 T^{-1}]$","$[L^2 T^{-2}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The argument of sine must be dimensionless, so $[B][T] = 1$, which gives $[B] = [T^{-1}]$.\nEach term must have the dimension of $x$, so $[A] = [L]$.\nFrom $[C][T^2] = [L]$, we get $[C] = [LT^{-2}]$.\nThen $\\frac{AC}{B} = \\frac{[L][LT^{-2}]}{[T^{-1}]} = [L^2T^{-1}]$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) 3.48 m + 2.1 m reported as 5.6 m\n(B) 4.20 m × 1.5 m reported as 6.3 m$^2$\n(C) 7.46 g − 7.41 g reported as 0.05 g\n(D) Percentage error in a mass of $0.50 \\pm 0.01$ g\nList-II\n(P) Subtraction of nearly equal values leaves only one significant figure\n(Q) Result rounded to the fewest decimal places among the data\n(R) Result keeps the fewest significant figures among the factors\n(S) 2 %","image":"","options":["A-Q, B-R, C-P, D-S","A-R, B-Q, C-P, D-S","A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P","A-P, B-R, C-Q, D-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) is addition: 5.58 m is rounded to one decimal place (set by 2.1 m), giving 5.6 m → Q.\n(B) is multiplication: 6.30 m$^2$ keeps 2 significant figures (set by 1.5 m), giving 6.3 m$^2$ → R.\n(C) Both values have 3 significant figures, but the difference 0.05 g has only one, showing that subtraction reduces significant figures → P.\n(D) $\\frac{0.01}{0.50} \\times 100 = 2$ % → S.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The position of a particle is given by $x = A\\sin(Bt) + Ct^2$, where $x$ is in metres and $t$ is in seconds. What are the dimensions of $\\frac{AB}{C}$?","image":"","options":["$[M^0 L^0 T]$","$[M^0 L^0 T^{-1}]$","$[M^0 L T]$","$[M^0 L^0 T^0]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The argument of sine must be dimensionless. So $[B][T]=1$, which gives $[B]=[T^{-1}]$.\nEach term must have the dimensions of $x$, by the principle of homogeneity:\n- $[A]=[L]$, since $\\sin(Bt)$ is dimensionless.\n- $[C][T^2]=[L]$, so $[C]=[LT^{-2}]$.\nThen $\\left[\\frac{AB}{C}\\right]=\\frac{[L][T^{-1}]}{[LT^{-2}]}=[T]$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Using the NCERT rounding convention, the numbers 6.235 and 6.245 are each rounded off to three significant figures. The results are respectively:","image":"","options":["6.23 and 6.24","6.24 and 6.25","6.24 and 6.24","6.23 and 6.25"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rule: if the digit to be dropped is exactly 5, look at the digit before it. If that digit is even, simply drop the 5. If it is odd, raise it by 1.\n6.235: the preceding digit 3 is odd, so it becomes 4, giving 6.24.\n6.245: the preceding digit 4 is even, so the 5 is dropped, giving 6.24.\nAlways rounding 5 upward would wrongly give 6.25 for the second number.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (constant in an equation) with List-II (its dimensional formula). Here $t$ is time, $x$ is distance, $v$ is velocity and $F$ is force.\nList-I:\n(A) $\\alpha$ in $y = A e^{-\\alpha t}$\n(B) $\\beta$ in $\\log(\\beta x)$\n(C) $\\gamma$ in $F = \\gamma v$\n(D) $\\delta$ in $x = x_0 + \\delta t^3$\nList-II:\n(i) $[L^{-1}]$\n(ii) $[T^{-1}]$\n(iii) $[M T^{-1}]$\n(iv) $[L T^{-3}]$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The exponent $\\alpha t$ must be dimensionless, so $[\\alpha] = [T^{-1}]$. This is (ii).\n(B) The log argument $\\beta x$ must be dimensionless, so $[\\beta] = [L^{-1}]$. This is (i).\n(C) $[\\gamma] = [MLT^{-2}]/[LT^{-1}] = [MT^{-1}]$. This is (iii).\n(D) $\\delta t^3$ must be a length, so $[\\delta] = [LT^{-3}]$. This is (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about plane angle and solid angle.\n(i) Plane angle $d\\theta$ is the ratio of arc length $ds$ to radius $r$, and its unit is the radian.\n(ii) Solid angle $d\\Omega$ is the ratio of the intercepted spherical area $dA$ to the square of the radius, and its unit is the steradian.\n(iii) Both radian and steradian carry the dimension of length.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(i) and (iii) only","(ii) and (iii) only","(i), (ii) and (iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $d\\theta = \\frac{ds}{r}$, and its unit is rad.\n(ii) Correct: $d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$, and its unit is sr.\n(iii) Incorrect: each is a ratio of like quantities (length/length or area/area), so both are dimensionless.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A length reported as 4.700 m has the same number of significant figures when it is expressed as 0.004700 km.\nReason (R): Changing the unit in which a measurement is expressed does not change the number of significant figures in it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. 4.700 m has four significant figures (4, 7, 0, 0). In 0.004700 km, the leading zeros are not significant and the trailing zeros after the decimal point are significant, so it also has four.\nR is true. Significant figures reflect the precision of the instrument, and changing the unit does not change that precision.\nThe principle stated in R is exactly why A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Measuring a physical quantity means comparing it with a basic, arbitrarily chosen, internationally accepted reference standard. This reference standard is called a:","image":"","options":["dimension","unit","significant figure","order of magnitude"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says measurement is a comparison with an accepted reference standard, and that standard is called a unit.\nThe result of a measurement is therefore a number (the numerical measure) together with a unit.\nA dimension describes the nature of a quantity, not the standard used for comparison.\nSignificant figures and order of magnitude describe how a value is reported.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A car moves at a constant speed of 72 km h$^{-1}$. How far does it travel in 3.0 s?","image":"","options":["60 m","216 m","20 m","24 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v = 72$ km h$^{-1}$, $t = 3.0$ s.\nConversion: 1 km h$^{-1}$ = $\\frac{1000}{3600}$ m s$^{-1}$ = $\\frac{5}{18}$ m s$^{-1}$, so $v = 72 \\times \\frac{5}{18} = 20$ m s$^{-1}$.\nDistance: $d = vt = 20 \\times 3.0$.\nSo d = 60 m. Option (2) comes from forgetting to convert the units.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Work and torque have the same dimensional formula $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$.\nStatement II: Dimensional analysis can be used to decide whether a given expression of dimensions $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$ represents work or torque.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Work = force × displacement and torque = force × perpendicular distance. Both are $[MLT^{-2}][L] = [ML^2T^{-2}]$.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT notes that the method of dimensions cannot distinguish between physical quantities that have the same dimensions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A rectangular metal plate has length 3.125 m, breadth 0.804 m and thickness 1.5 cm. Which option gives its area (length × breadth) and its volume to correct significant figures?","image":"","options":["2.513 $m^2$ and 0.0377 $m^3$","2.51 $m^2$ and 0.0377 $m^3$","2.5 $m^2$ and 0.038 $m^3$","2.51 $m^2$ and 0.038 $m^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: length has 4 significant figures, breadth has 3, and thickness 1.5 cm = 0.015 m has 2.\nArea = 3.125 × 0.804 = 2.5125 $m^2$. Keep 3 significant figures (limited by breadth): 2.51 $m^2$.\nVolume = 2.5125 × 0.015 = 0.0376875 $m^3$. Keep 2 significant figures (limited by thickness): 0.038 $m^3$.\nOption 2 ignores the thickness, which limits the volume to 2 significant figures.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Besides the seven SI base units, units are defined for plane angle (radian) and solid angle (steradian), and both these quantities are dimensionless.\nStatement II: Solid angle is defined as the ratio of the length of an arc to the radius of the circle.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT defines the radian (rad) for plane angle and the steradian (sr) for solid angle, and both are dimensionless.\nStatement II: Incorrect. Arc length divided by radius, $d\\theta = \\frac{ds}{r}$, defines the plane angle.\nSolid angle is $d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$, the intercepted area of the spherical surface divided by the square of its radius.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are SI base quantities?\nLength, force, electric current, luminous intensity, plane angle, amount of substance, energy, thermodynamic temperature","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The seven SI base quantities are length, mass, time, electric current, thermodynamic temperature, amount of substance and luminous intensity.\nFrom the list, these five are base quantities: length, electric current, luminous intensity, amount of substance and thermodynamic temperature.\nForce and energy are derived quantities.\nPlane angle is a dimensionless quantity with its own unit (rad), but it is not a base quantity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A beaker with salt has a mass of 25.4 g. The empty beaker has a mass of 13.17 g. Following the rules for significant figures, how should the mass of the salt be reported?","image":"","options":["12.23 g","12.230 g","12 g","12.2 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Direct subtraction gives 25.4 g − 13.17 g = 12.23 g.\nIn addition and subtraction, the result keeps as many decimal places as the least precise value. 25.4 g has only one decimal place.\nSo the result is rounded to one decimal place, giving 12.2 g.\n12.23 g claims more precision than the data supports. 12 g wrongly applies a rule meant for multiplication and division, and it also loses valid precision.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"What is the dimensional formula of mass density?","image":"","options":["$[M L^{3} T^{0}]$","$[M L^{-3} T^{0}]$","$[M L^{-2} T^{0}]$","$[M^{-1} L^{3} T^{0}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mass density = mass/volume.\nThe dimension of mass is [M] and the dimension of volume is $[L^3]$.\nSo $[\\rho] = [M]/[L^3] = [M L^{-3} T^0]$.\nOption (4) is the dimension of volume per unit mass, which is the inverse quantity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A distance is measured with a device whose readings are reliable up to the hundreds digit, while the tens digit is the first uncertain digit. The reading obtained is fifteen hundred metres. Which of the following is the correct, unambiguous way to report it?","image":"","options":["1500 m","$1.5 \\times 10^{3}$ m","$1.50 \\times 10^{3}$ m","$1.500 \\times 10^{3}$ m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The reported value must contain the reliable digits plus the first uncertain digit. Here these are 1 and 5 (reliable) and 0 in the tens place (uncertain), which is 3 significant figures.\nWriting 1500 m implies only 2 significant figures, since trailing zeros without a decimal point are not significant. This understates the precision.\n$1.5 \\times 10^{3}$ shows 2 figures and $1.500 \\times 10^{3}$ shows 4, which wrongly claims a reliable tens digit.\nScientific notation with exactly three digits, $1.50 \\times 10^{3}$ m, removes the ambiguity.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the dimensional consistency test of an equation is correct?","image":"","options":["An equation that passes the dimensional test is proved to be exactly correct.","An equation that fails the dimensional test must be wrong.","The dimensional test can find the numerical constants in an equation.","The dimensional test can tell apart two quantities having the same dimensions."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: passing the test does not prove an equation right. Dimensionless constants or functions may still be wrong.\n(2) Correct: a dimensionally inconsistent equation must be wrong.\n(3) Incorrect: dimensionless constants cannot be obtained by this method.\n(4) Incorrect: the method cannot distinguish quantities with the same dimensions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A student measures the length of a pencil as $4.00 \\pm 0.02$ cm. What is the percentage error in this measurement?","image":"","options":["0.05 %","0.2 %","0.5 %","2 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: measured value = 4.00 cm, absolute error = 0.02 cm.\nFormula: percentage error = (absolute error / measured value) × 100 %.\nSubstituting: (0.02 / 4.00) × 100 % = 0.5 %.\nSo the percentage error = 0.5 %. The option 2 % comes from forgetting to divide by the measured value.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Dimensional analysis cannot decide whether the kinetic energy of a body is $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ or $\\frac{3}{16}mv^2$.\nReason (R): Dimensionless constants cannot be obtained by the method of dimensions.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: both expressions have dimensions $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$, so both pass the homogeneity test.\nR is true: numerical factors such as $\\frac{1}{2}$ or $2\\pi$ carry no dimensions, so the method of dimensions cannot fix them.\nThe two formulas differ only in such a constant, so R directly explains A. The correct factor comes from the definition of kinetic energy.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The radian and the steradian are units, yet plane angle and solid angle are dimensionless quantities.\nReason (R): Plane angle is defined as the ratio of arc length to radius, and solid angle is defined as the ratio of intercepted area to the square of the radius.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that rad and sr are units, but both quantities are dimensionless.\nR is true: $d\\theta = \\frac{ds}{r}$ and $d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$.\nR explains A. In $\\frac{ds}{r}$ the dimensions are [L]/[L], and in $\\frac{dA}{r^2}$ they are [L$^2$]/[L$^2$]. In both cases the dimensions cancel, so the angle is a pure number even though it carries a named unit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A new system of units is defined in which the unit of mass is 100 g, the unit of length is 10 cm and the unit of time is 0.1 s. A pressure of $1.0\\times10^{5}$ kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$ in this new system has a numerical value of:","image":"","options":["$1.0\\times10^{3}$","$1.0\\times10^{7}$","$1.0\\times10^{1}$","$1.0\\times10^{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: new units are 0.1 kg (mass), 0.1 m (length) and 0.1 s (time).\nFormula: new unit of pressure = (0.1 kg)(0.1 m)$^{-1}$(0.1 s)$^{-2}$.\nSubstituting: new unit = $\\frac{0.1}{0.1\\times0.01}$ = 100 kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$.\nNumerical value = $\\frac{1.0\\times10^5}{100}$.\nSo the value is $1.0\\times10^{3}$ new units.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A screw gauge of pitch 1 mm with 100 divisions on the circular scale has a least count of 0.01 mm.\n(ii) A vernier calliper in which 20 vernier divisions coincide with 19 main scale divisions of 1 mm each has a least count of 0.05 mm.\n(iii) The precision of a measurement, indicated by its significant figures, depends on the least count of the measuring instrument.\n(iv) Converting a length from centimetre to millimetre increases its number of significant figures.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: least count = 1 mm/100 = 0.01 mm.\n(ii) Correct: least count = 1 MSD − 1 VSD = 1 mm − (19/20) mm = 0.05 mm.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT states that significant figures indicate precision, which depends on the instrument's least count.\n(iv) Incorrect: a change of units does not change the number of significant figures.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The steradian is the unit of solid angle. A solid angle is the ratio of the area intercepted on a sphere drawn about the apex to the square of that sphere's radius.\nStatement II: Both plane angle and solid angle are dimensionless quantities.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT defines the solid angle as $d\\Omega = \\frac{dA}{r^2}$. Here dA is the area intercepted on a sphere centred at the apex. Its unit is the steradian (sr).\nStatement II is correct: the plane angle $d\\theta = \\frac{ds}{r}$ is length/length. The solid angle is area/area. So both carry no dimensions, even though they have the units rad and sr.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (measured value) with List-II (number of significant figures).\nList-I:\n(A) 6.032 N\n(B) 0.0006 m\n(C) $5.00 \\times 10^{-3}$ kg\n(D) 1200 m (written without a decimal point)\nList-II:\n(I) 1\n(II) 2\n(III) 3\n(IV) 4","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) 6.032: all non-zero digits count, and the zero between non-zero digits also counts. That gives 4 (IV).\n(B) 0.0006: the leading zeros in a number less than 1 are not significant, so only the 6 counts. That gives 1 (I).\n(C) $5.00 \\times 10^{-3}$: all digits in the base number are significant, and the power of 10 does not matter. That gives 3 (III).\n(D) 1200 with no decimal point: the trailing zeros are not significant. That gives 2 (II).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The sum of the masses 25.4 g and 3.162 g should be reported as 28.6 g and not as 28.56 g.\nReason (R): In addition, the result keeps as many significant figures as the number with the fewest significant figures.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: 25.4 + 3.162 = 28.562 g. The least precise term, 25.4 g, has only one decimal place, so the sum is 28.6 g. A is true.\nStep 2: R states the rule for multiplication and division. For addition, the rule is about the fewest decimal places, not the fewest significant figures. R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following measured values have exactly four significant figures?\n3.500 s, 0.06900 kg, 12300 m, $4.700 \\times 10^{-3}$ km, 0.0230 m","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"3.500: trailing zeros after a decimal point are significant, so it has 4 significant figures.\n0.06900: leading zeros are not significant, trailing zeros are, so it has 4 (6, 9, 0, 0).\n12300: trailing zeros without a decimal point are not significant, so it has 3.\n$4.700 \\times 10^{-3}$: all digits in the base number are significant, so it has 4.\n0.0230: the significant digits are 2, 3, 0, so it has 3.\nThree of the values have four significant figures.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the number 0.002308, the two zeros immediately after the decimal point are not significant, but the zero between 3 and 8 is significant.\nStatement II: In a number less than 1, such as 0.1250, the zero conventionally written to the left of the decimal point is significant.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. For a number less than 1, the zeros to the right of the decimal point but before the first non-zero digit are not significant. A zero lying between two non-zero digits is always significant, so 0.002308 has 4 significant figures.\nStatement II is incorrect. The 0 placed to the left of the decimal point in a number less than 1 is never significant. The trailing zero in 0.1250, however, is significant.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Two masses, 2.05 g and 8.95 g, are each measured to an accuracy of $\\pm 0.01$ g. Which of the following statements about their relative errors is correct?","image":"","options":["Both have the same relative error because their absolute errors are equal","The 2.05 g measurement has the larger relative error, about 0.5 % compared with about 0.1 %","The 8.95 g measurement has the larger relative error because it is the larger value","Relative error depends only on the number of significant figures, so both are equal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Relative error = absolute error ÷ measured value.\nFor 2.05 g: $\\frac{0.01}{2.05} \\times 100 \\approx 0.5$ %.\nFor 8.95 g: $\\frac{0.01}{8.95} \\times 100 \\approx 0.1$ %.\nNCERT notes that the relative error depends not only on the number of significant figures but also on the number itself. Both values have three significant figures, yet their relative errors differ, so options (1) and (4) fail.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following quantities are dimensionless?\nplane angle, solid angle, refractive index, strain, mass density, momentum","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plane angle = arc length/radius, which is dimensionless.\nSolid angle = area/(radius)$^2$, which is dimensionless.\nRefractive index = ratio of two speeds, which is dimensionless.\nStrain = change in length/original length, which is dimensionless.\nMass density has dimensions $[M L^{-3}]$, so it is not dimensionless.\nMomentum has dimensions $[M L T^{-1}]$, so it is not dimensionless.\nThat makes 4 dimensionless quantities.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The measured value 0.06900 m has four significant figures.\nReason (R): The trailing zeros in a number with a decimal point are significant, while the zeros to the left of the first non-zero digit in a number less than 1 are not.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In 0.06900, the leading zeros (0.0) are not significant. They only fix the decimal position.\nThe digits 6 and 9 are significant, and so are the two trailing zeros, because the number has a decimal point.\nThis gives four significant figures, so A is true.\nR states exactly these two rules, so R is true and correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The number of significant figures in 0.06900 is","image":"","options":["5","4","2","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rule 1: zeros to the left of the first non-zero digit are not significant. So the leading zeros in 0.0 are not counted.\nRule 2: trailing zeros after the decimal point are significant. So both final zeros in 6900 count.\nThe significant digits are 6, 9, 0, 0, which makes four.\nWhy not (1): the leading zeros only fix the decimal place.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 2 (MCQ Q2.1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Each of the following numbers is rounded off to three significant figures using the NCERT rounding convention:\n(i) 4.267 → 4.27\n(ii) 5.635 → 5.64\n(iii) 7.825 → 7.83\n(iv) 6.314 → 6.31\n(v) 9.455 → 9.46\nHow many of these roundings are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the dropped digit is 7, which is more than 5, so 6 is raised to 7.\n(ii) Correct: the dropped digit is 5 and the preceding digit 3 is odd, so 3 is raised to 4.\n(iii) Incorrect: the dropped digit is 5 and the preceding digit 2 is even, so the 5 is simply dropped. The correct result is 7.82.\n(iv) Correct: the dropped digit is 4, which is less than 5, so the value is unchanged at 6.31.\n(v) Correct: the dropped digit is 5 and the preceding digit 5 is odd, so it is raised to 6.\nFour roundings are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":169,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following results is NOT reported to the correct significant figures or decimal places?","image":"","options":["25.62 g + 3.4 g + 0.115 g = 29.1 g","3.2 m × 4.567 m = 15 m$^2$","15.8 s − 9.43 s = 6.37 s","7.50 g ÷ 2.5 cm$^3$ = 3.0 g cm$^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: for addition, keep the least number of decimal places (one, from 3.4). So 29.135 becomes 29.1 g.\n(2) Correct: for multiplication, keep the least number of significant figures (two, from 3.2). So 14.6144 becomes 15 m².\n(3) Incorrect: 15.8 has only one decimal place, so the difference 6.37 must be rounded to 6.4 s.\n(4) Correct: 2.5 has two significant figures, so the quotient is 3.0 g cm⁻³.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":170,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A length reported as 4.700 m has four significant figures, and the same length expressed as 4700 mm still carries four significant figures.\nReason (R): A mere change of units cannot change the number of significant figures in a measurement.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The trailing zeros in 4.700 m are written on purpose to show the precision, so they are significant and the value has four significant figures. Converting to 4700 mm does not change how precise the measurement is.\nR is true. The precision depends on the least count of the instrument, not on the unit chosen. So changing units cannot alter the number of significant figures.\nR directly explains A. The ambiguity in writing 4700 is only a notation problem, and scientific notation (4.700 × 10^3 mm) removes it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":171,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Two rods measure 0.507 m and 0.503 m. How many significant figures does their correctly reported difference have?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Both lengths have 3 decimal places, so the difference is also kept to 3 decimal places.\nStep 2: 0.507 − 0.503 = 0.004 m, which is $4 \\times 10^{-3}$ m.\nStep 3: The zeros before 4 are not significant, so only one significant figure remains.\nStep 4: Writing 0.00400 m to keep 3 significant figures would wrongly suggest higher precision. Subtraction can reduce the number of significant figures.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":172,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A measured length is computed as 6.283 cm. What is this value when rounded off to three significant figures?","image":"","options":["6.28 cm","6.29 cm","6.30 cm","6.2 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Keeping three significant figures means the digit to be dropped is 3.\nRule: if the digit to be dropped is less than 5, the preceding digit is left unchanged.\nSo 6.283 becomes 6.28 cm.\n6.29 would require the dropped digit to be more than 5. 6.2 has only two significant figures.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":173,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"In the following, $m$ is mass, $v$ is speed, $p$ is linear momentum, $a$ is acceleration, $t$ is time and $x$ is distance. How many of these expressions can be ruled out as a formula for kinetic energy purely on dimensional grounds?\n(i) $K = \\frac{p^2}{2m}$\n(ii) $K = m a t$\n(iii) $K = \\frac{1}{2} m v^2 + m v$\n(iv) $K = m a x$\n(v) $K = \\frac{3}{4} m v^2$\n(vi) $K = m^2 v$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Kinetic energy has dimensions $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$.\n(i) $[M^2 L^2 T^{-2}]/[M] = [M L^2 T^{-2}]$: passes.\n(ii) $[M][L T^{-2}][T] = [M L T^{-1}]$: ruled out.\n(iii) Adds $[M L^2 T^{-2}]$ to $[M L T^{-1}]$, which are unlike quantities: ruled out.\n(iv) $[M][L T^{-2}][L] = [M L^2 T^{-2}]$: passes.\n(v) Passes. It is dimensionally fine even though the numerical factor is wrong.\n(vi) $[M^2 L T^{-1}]$: ruled out.\nSo three expressions are ruled out.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":174,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The base units of length, mass and time in the MKS system are the same as those in SI.\nStatement II: The radian and the steradian are two of the seven base units of SI.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: MKS uses metre, kilogram and second, which are also the SI units of length, mass and time.\nStatement II is incorrect: the radian (plane angle) and steradian (solid angle) are defined in addition to the seven base units.\nBoth the radian and the steradian are dimensionless.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":175,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"A small liquid drop oscillates under surface tension. Assume its frequency of oscillation f depends only on three quantities: the drop's radius r, the liquid's density $\\rho$, and the surface tension S (force per unit length). Using the method of dimensions, f is proportional to:","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{\\frac{S}{\\rho r^{3}}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{S r}{\\rho}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{\\rho r^{3}}{S}}$","$\\frac{S}{\\rho r^{2}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write $f = k\\, r^a \\rho^b S^c$, where k is a dimensionless constant.\nDimensions: $[f] = [T^{-1}]$, $[\\rho] = [M L^{-3}]$, and $[S] = [M L T^{-2}]/[L] = [M T^{-2}]$.\nEquate the powers of each base quantity:\nM: $b + c = 0$\nL: $a - 3b = 0$\nT: $-2c = -1$\nSolving gives $c = \\frac{1}{2}$, $b = -\\frac{1}{2}$, $a = -\\frac{3}{2}$.\nSo $f \\propto \\sqrt{\\frac{S}{\\rho r^3}}$. Option (3) is its reciprocal, which has the dimensions of time.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":176,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"According to the principle of homogeneity of dimensions, which of the following pairs of quantities can be meaningfully added together?","image":"","options":["Velocity and force","Change in velocity and initial velocity","Electric current and thermodynamic temperature","Mass and length"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only quantities with the same dimensions can be added or subtracted.\nChange in velocity and initial velocity both have dimensions $[L T^{-1}]$, so they can be added.\nVelocity $[LT^{-1}]$ and force $[MLT^{-2}]$ have different dimensions. Current [A] and temperature [K] are different base quantities, and so are mass [M] and length [L].\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-UNITS-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Units and Measurements","qno":177,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Units and Measurements","type":"mcq","question":"The position of a particle moving along a straight line is given by $x = \\frac{a}{b}\\left(1 - e^{-bt}\\right)$, where $t$ is time and $a$, $b$ are constants. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$b$ has the dimensions $[T^{-1}]$","$a$ has the dimensions of velocity","$ab^2$ has the dimensions of acceleration","$\\frac{a}{b}$ has the dimensions of length"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The exponent $bt$ must be dimensionless, so $[b] = [T^{-1}]$. Statement (1) is correct.\nThe bracket $(1 - e^{-bt})$ is dimensionless, so $[a/b] = [L]$. Statement (4) is correct.\nThen $[a] = [L][T^{-1}] = [LT^{-1}]$, which is velocity. Statement (2) is correct.\n$[ab^2] = [LT^{-1}][T^{-2}] = [LT^{-3}]$, not acceleration $[LT^{-2}]$. Statement (3) is incorrect. (It is $ab$ that has the dimensions of acceleration.)\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 1 · Units and Measurements","difficulty":"hard"}]});
